\subsection{Recovering Elections with Condorcet Winners}\label{sec:app:condorcet}
Naturally, even matrices that do contain zero entries may correspond
to elections with fairly different features. As an example, let us
consider the notion of a Condorcet winner: A candidate $c$ is a
Condorcet winner in election $E = (C,V)$ if for every candidate
$d \in C \setminus \{c\}$ it holds that a majority of the voters
prefers $c$ to $d$.
Given a position matrix $X$, it is natural to ask if there is an
election that induces it and that does have a Condorcet winner.
If this is the case, then we say that $X$ is a Condorcet matrix, and
otherwise we say that it is a non-Condorcet matrix (we use analogous
terminology for elections with and without Condorcet winners).

While it is unclear if there is a polynomial-time algorithm for
deciding if a given matrix is a Condorcet matrix, we have expressed this decision problem
as an
integer linear program (ILP) and solved it for the position matrices
of the elections in the $10 \times 100$ and $20 \times 100$ datasets of
\citet{szu-fal-sko-sli-tal:c:map}.  We naturally
distinguish three cases: Condorcet matrices generated
from Condorcet elections (note that by definition all Condorcet elections generate Condorcet matrices), Condorcet matrices generated from
non-Condorcet elections (in this case our ILP found an
election with a Condorcet winner and the same position matrix as the given non-Condorcet election), and
non-Condorcet matrices from
non-Condorcet elections (in this case our ILP returned that there exists no Condorcet election with the same position matrix as the given non-Condorcet election). We show the results in
Table~\ref{tab:Condorcet}.

\begin{table}
  \centering
  \resizebox{0.48\textwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{l|cc}
    \toprule
    & $10 \times 100$ & $20 \times 100$ \\
    \midrule
    Cond. matrices from Cond. elections  & 564 & 494 \\
    Cond. matrices from non-Cond. elections  & 235 & 305 \\
    non-Cond. matrices from non-Cond. elections  & 1 & 1 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}}
  \caption{\label{tab:Condorcet} Overview of the number of elections from the $10 \times 100$ and
    $20 \times 100$ datasets of \citet{szu-fal-sko-sli-tal:c:map} with/without Condorcet winner for which an/no election with identical position matrix that admits a Condorcet winner exists.}
\end{table}

As we can see, more than half of the elections of
\citet{szu-fal-sko-sli-tal:c:map} already admitted Condorcet winners
and nearly all the other elections corresponded to Condorcet matrices.
While the large number of Condorcet matrices from non-Condorcet elections does show some limitations of the positionwise
distance---after all, having or not having a Condorcet winner is often
viewed as a rather basic feature of an election---this limitation is
quite expected as the relative rankings and pairwise comparisons of the candidates are not captured by the positionwise distance.

