\end{figure}

Yet, even the positionwise distance preserves some structural
information pertaining to Condorcet winners.  Given a
position matrix $X$, let $c_1, \ldots, c_m$ be the candidates that
corresponds to its rows, and let the Condorcet number of $X$ be the
number of different candidates $c_i$ for which there is an election with $X$ as its position matrix and $c_i$ as its Condorcet winner.
In Figure~\ref{fi:CondFreq-map} we show the map of elections for the $10 \times 100$ dataset from \citet{szu-fal-sko-sli-tal:c:map},
where the color of each point visualises the Condorcet number
of the corresponding matrix. The map shows clear clusters of matrices
with similar Condorcet numbers.
For instance, on the right, we have matrices with
only few different Condorcet winners (which is also quite intuitive
because matrices there are typically similar to identity). Moving from the right to the left, the number
of different Condorcet winners first increases (in the region where
elections typically have some but not a very strong structure) and
then decreases again for very unstructured elections close to uniformity.

Moreover, we depict in \Cref{fi:CondFreq-line} the number of position
matrices with some Condorcet number for a varying number of voters. We
used the $10 \times 100$ dataset from
\citet{szu-fal-sko-sli-tal:c:map} to generate this figure. For
$x\in \{10,20,\dots, 90,100\}$ voters, we created a separate dataset
by drawing a subset of $x$ votes from each election from Szufa et
al.'s dataset. Examining the figure, firstly, note that with an
increasing number of voters the number of different Condorcet winners
increases. Recalling our observation from \Cref{se:recov} concerning
non-zero entries in position matrices, this is quite intuitive, as
with an increasing number of voters the smallest entry in a position
matrix tends to increase. Secondly, we observe that the considered
matrices typically have a Condorcet number between one and five. The
fact that almost half of all matrices admit four or more different
Condorcet winners is quite striking and highlights that pairwise
comparisons are not directly captured by position matrices.

