We mention that instead of defining monotonicity for triples of
elections, we could have defined it for quadruples. Given elections
$X$ and $Y$ and elections $A$ and $B$, we could ask if the fact that
the original distance between $X$ and $Y$ is smaller than the original
distance between $A$ and $B$ implies the same relation between the
embedded distances. This would have lead to a somewhat more accurate
analysis, but would require significant amount of computation without
providing a clear advantage (in each of our datasets, we would have to analyze
nearly $480^4 \approx 53\cdot 10^9$ quadruples of elections).

