\subsection{Finding the Hyperparameters}
To decide the parameters going into the dataset generation, we fix the parameters for the normal patterns as shown in the \emph{Normal} column of Table~\ref{tab:data_parameters} by using publicly available information from the Swish mobile payment system~\cite{swish2023downtime}.
We then optimize the money laundering parameters, i.e., the mean and variance of the transaction size distribution, the probability of spending cash if available, the probability to participate in multiple alert patterns, and the parameters going into the phone and bank changes, respectively,  by means of Bayesian optimization using \textsc{Optuna}.
As an objective, we make use of the average precision for recall larger than $0.6$, and target an FPR of $0.98$ when forced to use a decision tree classifier. 
This value is chosen based on reported values from the literature and aims to create a dataset that is very challenging for classical methods, see, e.g.,~\cite{pwc2024aml}.
The resulting parameters for the money laundering patterns are shown in Table~\ref{tab:data_parameters} under column \emph{Money laundering}.

