\begin{abstract}
% Traditional approaches struggle with scalability, adaptability, and seamless resource integration within dynamic and complex environments.
% This paper introduces an intelligent holonic architecture enhanced by Large Language Models (LLMs) that enables autonomous decision-making, real-time plan validation, and dynamic resource allocation.
% % In this architecture, LLMs enable adaptive trip planning, customer interaction, and seamless resource integration, enhancing the flexibility of holonic systems. 
% LLMs enhance holonic flexibility by enabling adaptive trip planning, customer interaction, and resource integration.
% This approach supports ad-hoc scalability through plug-and-operate functionality, dynamic task allocation, and optimized resource management, making UAM systems more responsive and resilient. 
% A case study demonstrates improved responsiveness and resilience in multimodal transportation coordination, highlighting the framework's potential for intelligent UAM operations.

Urban Air Mobility (UAM) is an emerging System of System (SoS) that faces challenges in system architecture, planning, task management, and execution. 
Traditional architectural approaches struggle with scalability, adaptability, and seamless resource integration within dynamic and complex environments.
This paper presents an intelligent holonic architecture that incorporates Large Language Model (LLM) to manage the complexities of UAM. Holons function semi-autonomously, allowing for real-time coordination among air taxis, ground transport, and vertiports. LLMs process natural language inputs, generate adaptive plans, and manage disruptions such as weather changes or airspace closures.
Through a case study of multimodal transportation with electric scooters and air taxis, we demonstrate how this architecture enables dynamic resource allocation, real-time replanning, and autonomous adaptation without centralized control, creating more resilient and efficient urban transportation networks.
By advancing decentralized control and AI-driven adaptability, this work lays the groundwork for resilient, human-centric UAM ecosystems, with future efforts targeting hybrid AI integration and real-world validation.




\end{abstract}

