\subsection{Auto-bidding Process}
As shown in \cref{fig:auto_bidding_process}, a typical auto-bidding process usually involves four components, including advertisers who use the auto-bidding service, the agents created and designed by the platform, an auction mechanism and impression opportunities.  Suppose that there are $N\in\mathbb{N}_+$ advertisers utilizing the auto-bidding service to bid for impression opportunities (where $N$ is typically very large, e.g., $10^3\sim 10^6$).
Each advertiser $i\in [N]$ is self-interested and wishes to maximize their expected return (i.e., the total value of the impression opportunities won) within a budget $B_i\in\mathbb{R}_+$ throughout the bidding episode (e.g., a day). 
The platform then creates an agent for each advertiser to bid for impression opportunities on their behalf. Specifically, the bidding episode is divided into $T\in\mathbb{N}_+$ time steps and each agent $i$ bids at $a_{i,t}\in\mathcal{A}$ for all impression opportunities between time steps $t$ and $t+1$, where $\mathcal{A}$ denotes the bid space and $t\in[T]$.
The policy of each agent is meticulously designed by the platform (to achieve a certain purpose as we propose in \cref{sec:prob_form}).

Let the contextual feature spaces \footnote{The contextual feature of an advertiser can contain the categories of their products, their target buyers, etc. The contextual feature of an impression opportunity can contain its context information, the user characteristics, etc.} of the advertiser and impression opportunity be $\mathcal{X}$ and $\mathcal{Y}$, respectively. 
The auction mechanism for each impression opportunity can typically be modeled as a combination of an \emph{allocation mechanism} $g:\mathcal{X}^N\times \mathcal{A}^N\times\mathcal{Y}\rightarrow \{0,1\}^N$ and a \emph{pricing mechanism} $u:\mathcal{X}^N\times\mathcal{A}^N\times\mathcal{Y}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^N_+$, respectively, where the $i$-th element in the output of $g$ represents the probability of winning for agent $i$, and the $i$-th element in the output of $u$ represents the cost of agent $i$. Without loss of generality \cite{qin2022benefits}, we can assume that $g$ and $u$ are both \emph{permutation-equivariant} functions, i.e.,


\begin{assumption}[Permutation Equivariant Auction Mechanism]
    \label{assump:equ_mechanism}
	For any permutation matrix $\rho_n$, we have $g(\rho_n\mathbf{x},\rho_n\mathbf{a}_{t},y)=\rho_n g(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{a}_{t},y)$ and $u(\rho_n\mathbf{x},\rho_n\mathbf{a}_{t},y)=\rho_n u(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{a}_{t},y)$, where $\mathbf{a}_t\triangleq a_{1:N,t}$, and $\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}^N$ and $y\in\mathcal{Y}$ denote the contextual features of all advertisers and the impression opportunity being auctioned, respectively.
\end{assumption}

%In this paper, we focus on the policy design of the agents from the perspective of the platform. The game between the agents in the auto-bidding process can be modeled as follows. 

\subsection{Game Model of Agents}
The auto-bidding process of all agents can be modeled as \emph{partially observed Markov game} (POMG) $<\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{A},  \phi, r, P >$, where ${s}_{i,t}\in\mathcal{S}$ represents the local state of agent $i$ at the time step $t$ (that includes all its relevant features, such as budget left, contextual features, etc.), and $\mathbf{s}_t\triangleq s_{1:N, t}\in\mathcal{S}^N$ denotes the global state of all agents.
Due to privacy concerns, agent $i$ is limited to observe only its local state $s_{i,t}$ rather than the global state $\mathbf{s}_t$.
Denote the initial global state distribution as $\phi\triangleq \phi_{1:N}$, where $\phi_i$ represents the initial local state distribution of the agent $i$.
Let the policy of agent $i$ be $\pi_i:\Omega\rightarrow\Delta(\mathcal{A})$.
At time step $t$, agent $i$ bids at $a_{i,t}\sim \pi_i(s_{i,t})$, receives the value of the impression opportunities won between time step $t$ and $t+1$ as the reward $r(\mathbf{s}_t,\mathbf{a}_t,i)$ and pays the corresponding cost $c_{i,t}$ 
according to the auction mechanism. 
The joint reward of all agents is denoted as $r(\mathbf{s}_t,\mathbf{a}_t)$.
Afterwards, all agents move to the next global state $\mathbf{s}_{t+1}$ according to the transition rule $P:\mathcal{S}^N\times\mathcal{A}^N\rightarrow\Delta(\mathcal{S}^N)$. 
Each agent repeats this process until the bidding episode ends or its budget runs out. 
%Denote the joint reward of all agents as $r(\mathbf{s}_t,\mathbf{a}_t)$.

\textbf{Policy Parameterization.}
To represent the policy of each agent, we construct a neural network $\pi_\theta$ with the agent's index and its local state as the inputs and the bid distribution as the output, i.e., $\pi_\theta:\Omega\times [N] \rightarrow \Delta(\mathcal{A})$ %\footnote{Note that in practical implementations, we usually map the index to an one-hot vector and use a specific part of the neural network in $\pi_\theta$ to transform the one-hot vector into an embedding.}
, where $\theta$ denotes the trainable parameters.
When all agents adopt $\pi_\theta$,
the expected return of agent $i$ during the entire bidding episode is denoted as $G_i(\theta)\triangleq \mathbb{E}_{\theta}[\sum_{t\in[T]}r(\mathbf{s}_t,\mathbf{a}_t,i)]$. The social welfare of all agents refers to the sum of their expected returns, i.e., $\sum_{i\in[N]} G_i(\theta)$.

\textbf{$\epsilon$-Nash Equilibrium ($\epsilon$-NE).} 
An $\epsilon$-NE is a joint policy where no individual agent can increase its expected return more than $\epsilon\in\mathbb{R}_+$ by unilaterally improving its policy, i.e., $G_i(\theta)\ge\max_{\pi_i}G_i(\pi_i;\theta)-\epsilon, \forall i$, where $G_i(\pi_i;\theta)$ denotes the expected return of agent $i$ when it follows policy $\pi_i$ and all other agents adopt $\pi_\theta$. We refer to $\max_{\pi_i} G_i(\pi_i;\theta)$ as the $i$-th unilateral optimization problem.



\section{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:prob_form}
In this section, we propose the \textbf{\emph{Nash-Equilibrium Constrained Bidding} (NCB) problem}, a new formulation of the auto-bidding problem from the perspective of the platform. Specifically, we argue that since each agent is assigned to its corresponding advertiser and bids on behalf of them, it should prioritize optimizing their performance to the greatest extent possible, ensuring their satisfaction. Otherwise, it may raise ethical concerns and lead to potential performance degradation (compared to not using the platform's auto-bidding service or using alternative auto-bidding products), thereby increasing the platform's risk of losing advertisers and their associated budgets.
This makes $\epsilon$-NE a necessary solution concept. Then, since there may be multiple $\epsilon$-NEs \cite{NES}, the platform should pick the one that maximizes a certain collective metric for the benefits of the platform.
Here, we use the social welfare of all agents as this collective metric to optimize the long-term value of the platform. Specifically, increasing social welfare contributes to a thriving platform ecosystem, which not only
generates more impression opportunities for advertisers, thus fostering their business growth,
but also attracts more budgets from them, boosting the platform’s long-term revenue. Based on these considerations, we propose the NCB problem, which optimizes the social welfare of all agents under the $\epsilon$-NE constraint:

\vspace{-1mm}
\begin{align}
	\text{(NCB):}\;\;\;\;&\underbrace{\max_{\theta}\;\; \sum_{i\in [N]}G_i(\theta)	}_{\text{maximize social welfare}}\label{equ:ncb}\\
	&\;\;\underbrace{\mathrm{s.t.}\quad G_i(\theta)\ge\max_{\pi_i}G_i(\pi_i;\theta)-\epsilon,\forall i. }_{\text{$\epsilon$-NE constraint}}\tag{\ref{equ:ncb}{a}}\label{equ:ncb_constraint}
	%\\	
	%&\qquad\qquad\qquad\sum_{t\in[T]}c_{i,t}\le B_i, \forall i, \tag{\ref{equ:opt}{b}}\label{equ:opt_b}
\end{align}

We note that the above formulation and its approaches proposed in the following sections are \textbf{also compatible with} other collective metrics acting as the objective function in \eqref{equ:ncb}, such as the total costs of all agents (which corresponds to the short-term revenue of the platform).  

