However, advanced ES algorithms are often tailored for matrix games or monotone games, either relying on strong assumptions or involving gradient iterations of all agents \cite{benenati2023optimal}, which are not suitable for the general POMG in the NCB problem with large populations of agents \footnote{See \cref{app:es} for a review of ES algorithms.}.
Hence, to solve the NCB problem, we design a \textbf{\emph{bi-level policy gradient framework} (BPG framework)} with {theoretical guarantees} under mild assumptions, where its computational complexity remains {independent} of the number of agents and the associated gradients are {straightforward} to compute. 
