\textbf{Challenges in deriving the gradient.} It is nontrivial to compute the gradient of the NCB problem.  As unilateral optimization problems $\max_{\pi_i} G_i(\pi_i;\theta),\forall i$ in the constraint \eqref{equ:ncb_constraint} involve the optimization variable $\theta$, the NCB is naturally a \textbf{\emph{bi-level optimization problem}} \cite{talbi2013taxonomy} with \eqref{equ:ncb} as the upper-level problem and unilateral optimization problems as the lower-level ones. In particular, %the value of
$\theta$ will affect the solutions to unilateral optimization problems, which will subsequently influence
$\theta$ itself. This complicates the gradient computation of the NCB problem.
Yet, more than that, unlike a standard bi-level optimization problem where several lower-level optimization problems act as independent constraints, many unilateral optimization problems are \textbf{coupled} with the constraints of the NCB problem. Note that the number of unilateral optimization problems is \textbf{proportional to} the number of advertisers, which can be very large in practice.
Therefore, most existing bi-level optimization algorithms cannot be directly applied to compute the gradient.
A brief introduction to the bi-level optimization problem and the specifics of the NCB problem
is given in \cref{app:bi_level_ncb}.

\textbf{Basic ideas of the BPG framework.}
To address these challenges, 
our basic idea is to decouple unilateral optimization problems from constraint \eqref{equ:ncb_constraint} by establishing a primal-dual update framework for the NCB problem.
Specifically, it alternates between primal domain update \eqref{equ:primal_domain_objective}, where unilateral optimization problems serve as independent constraints, and dual domain update \eqref{equ:dual_update}, which provides primal domain update with feedback on violations of the constraint \eqref{equ:ncb_constraint} to help satisfy it. While dual domain update is easy to perform, primal domain update still involves a bi-level optimization problem with many unilateral optimization problems as constraints. To perform the primal domain update, we transform it into a penalized single-level optimization problem \eqref{equ:single_level_} with an additional optimization variable $\bar{\nu}$, named \emph{unified optimizer}. 
Specifically, the
unified optimizer aims to approximate a \emph{unified solution} to all unilateral optimization problems. The feasibility of the unified solution is supported by the permutation equivariance property of POMG as proved in \cref{thm:pe}. By penalizing the distance between the unified optimizer and the unified solution, we can eliminate unilateral optimization problems in the constraint and equivalently turn the primal domain update into a single-level optimization problem \eqref{equ:single_level_} with theoretical guarantees (\cref{thm:equivalence}). The gradient of this single-level optimization problem, as given in \eqref{equ:final_gradient}, is \textbf{straightforward} to compute and involves only two gradients for $\theta$ and $\bar{\nu}$, respectively, which is \textbf{independent} of the number of agents.  
In this way, the BPG framework can provably and efficiently solve the NCB problem. 
