\section{Bi-level Policy Gradient Framework}
\label{sec:policy_gradient}
In this section, we describe the detailed derivation of the BPG framework and its theoretical foundations. 
Specifically, with the Lagrange function of \eqref{equ:ncb}, the NCB problem can be equivalently expressed as:
\begin{align}
	\label{equ:NCB_lagrange}
	\min_\theta\max_{\lambda\succeq 0}\;L(\theta,\lambda,{\nu_{\theta}})
\end{align}
where  
$\lambda\triangleq \lambda_{1:N}\succeq 0$ is the Lagrange factor, and 
$L(\theta,\lambda, \nu_{\theta})$ is the Lagrange function of \eqref{equ:ncb}: 
\begin{align}
	 L(\theta,\lambda, \nu_{\theta})\triangleq\sum_{i\in[N]}\bigg[\lambda_iG_i(\nu_{i,\theta};\theta)-(1+\lambda_i)G_i(\theta)-\lambda_i\epsilon\bigg].\notag
\end{align}
Here we use $\nu_{\theta}\triangleq\{\nu_{i,\theta}\}_{i=1}^N$ to represent the solutions \footnote{We suppose the solution to $\max_{\pi_i}G_i(\pi_i;\theta)$ is represented by a neural network, and $\nu_{i,\theta} $ is a vector of parameters of that neural network. For simplicity, we refer $\nu_{i,\theta}$ directly as the solution to $\max_{\pi_i}G_i(\pi_i;\theta)$.} to the unilateral optimization problems in constraint \eqref{equ:ncb_constraint}, i.e.,
\begin{align}
\label{equ:nu_i_theta}
\nu_{i,\theta}\in \arg\max_{\pi_i} {G}_i(\pi_i;\theta),\; \forall i.
\end{align}
A classical way to solve the min-max problem like \eqref{equ:NCB_lagrange}
is to leverage the primal-dual method that has convergence guarantees \cite{chow2018risk}. Similarly, we establish a primal-dual optimization framework for \eqref{equ:NCB_lagrange} as follows, where $\theta$ and $\lambda$ are updated alternatively in the primal domain and dual domain updates, respectively, until convergence:
\begin{align}
%	&\text{Repeat Until Convergence:}\notag\\
&\text{(Primal Domain Update): fix $\lambda$ and update $\theta$ by }\notag\\ 
	&\qquad\;\quad\theta\leftarrow \arg\min_\theta L(\theta,\lambda,\nu_\theta)
    \label{equ:primal_domain_objective}
    \\
    &\qquad\;\quad\quad\quad\;\mathrm{s.t.} \;\;\nu_{i,\theta}\in \arg\max_{\pi_i} {G}_i(\pi_i;\theta),\; \forall i.
\tag{\ref{equ:primal_domain_objective}{a}}\label{equ:primal_domain_constraint}
\\
	&\text{(Dual Domain Update): fix $\theta$ and update $\lambda$ by}\notag\\
	&\qquad\;\quad\lambda_i\leftarrow [\lambda_i+\alpha\nabla_{\lambda_i}L(\theta,\lambda,\nu_\theta)]_+, \forall i,
	\label{equ:dual_update}
\end{align}
where $\alpha>0$ is the step size. 
The dual domain update is easy to perform and understand since
\begin{align}
	\nabla_{\lambda_i}L(\theta,\lambda,\nu_\theta)=G_i(\nu_{i,\theta};\theta)-G_i(\theta)-\epsilon
\end{align}
is directly the violation degree of constraint \eqref{equ:ncb_constraint}. The more severe the violation of constraint \eqref{equ:ncb_constraint}, the larger the value of $\lambda_i$, 
which provides feedback to the primal domain update and in turn help to reduce the constraint violations.

However, the primal domain update is still hard to perform since it involves a bi-level optimization problem constrained by many unilateral optimization problems. Specifically, the gradient of the primal domain update, $\nabla_\theta L(\theta, \lambda,\nu_\theta)$, as given in \eqref{equ:original_gradient} \footnote{Note that we refer to $L_1(\theta)$ as the \emph{cooperative gradient} since it resembles the derivative of a weighted social welfare, and we refer to $L_s(\theta,\nu_\theta)$ and $L_g(\theta,\nu_\theta)$ as the \emph{competitive gradients} since they involve the derivatives of the maximum unilateral return.}, involves a complex term $\nabla_\theta \nu_{i,\theta}$. Calculating this term can be computationally demanding since its analytical expression contains second-order derivatives and matrix inversions (as deduced in \cref{app:second_order}).
Although we can view $\nabla_{\pi_i} G_i(\pi_i;\theta)|_{\pi_i=\nu_{i,\theta}}=0$ (since $\nu_{i,\theta}$ is the solution to the $i$-th unilateral optimization problem) to help avoid the calculation of $\nabla_\theta \nu_{i,\theta}$, it is hard (and/or time-consuming) to find the exact global or local solution to the $i$-th unilateral optimization problem, $\nu_{i,\theta}$, in practice. Ignoring the computation of $\nabla_\theta \nu_{i,\theta}$ while using an estimated solution to the $i$-th unilateral optimization problem can lead to the accumulation of gradient errors, potentially degrading the performance of the BPG framework. This risk is experimentally validated in \cref{sec:abalation_what_if}.
Moreover, computing the gradient of the primal domain update  in \eqref{equ:original_gradient} 
involves solving all $N$ unilateral optimization problems to obtain $\nu_{i,\theta}, \forall i$, which can be computational infeasible in practice since $N$ is very large.
\vspace{-0.7mm}
\begin{align}
\label{equ:original_gradient}
	\nabla_\theta L(\theta,\lambda,\nu_\theta)=&-\underbrace{\sum_{i\in[N]}(1+\lambda_i)\nabla_\theta G_i(\theta)}_{\triangleq \textcolor{blue}{L_1(\theta)}, \text{ cooperative gradient}}\notag\\
    &+\underbrace{\sum_{i\in [N]}\lambda_i\nabla_\theta G_i(\nu_{i,\theta};\theta)}_{\triangleq \textcolor{blue}{L_s(\theta, \nu_{\theta})}, \text{competitive gradient}}\notag\\
    &+\underbrace{\sum_{i\in [N]}\lambda_i(
\textcolor{blue}{\nabla_\theta\nu_{i,\theta}})^\top\nabla_{\pi_i} G_i(\pi_i;\theta)\bigg\vert_{\pi_i=\nu_{i,\theta}}}_{\triangleq \textcolor{blue}{L_g(\theta, \nu_{\theta})}, \text{competitive gradient}}
\end{align}

\vspace{-1.3mm}
In the following sections (\cref{sec:bpg_1} to \ref{sec:bpg_3}), we design an equivalent single-level optimization problem for the primal domain update with a unified optimizer. In this way, the primal domain update is much easier to perform, and its computational complexity is independent of the number of agents. We summarize the overall framework in \cref{sec:overall_bpg}.
\subsection{Penalized Single-level Primal Domain Update}
\label{sec:bpg_1}
Motivated by the penalization-based bi-level optimization algorithms \cite{shen2023penalty}, it is possible to transform the primal domain update into a single-level optimization problem by penalizing the degree to which $\nu_\theta$ violate the solutions to unilateral optimization problems. Specifically, instead of regarding $\nu_\theta$ as a function of $\theta$,
we treat it as an additional independent optimization variable and re-denote it as $\nu\triangleq\{\nu_i\}_{i=1}^N$. During the optimization of $\theta$ in the primal domain update, we simultaneously optimize $\nu_i$ to approximate the solution to the $i$-th unilateral optimization problem in order to satisfy the constraint \eqref{equ:primal_domain_constraint}.
Specifically, 
to realize this, we add an regularization term in \eqref{equ:primal_domain_objective} to penalize the gap between $\nu$ and the solutions to unilateral optimization problems.
In this way, the constraint \eqref{equ:primal_domain_constraint} is eliminated and the primal domain update is transformed into a \textbf{single-level optimization problem}:
\begin{align}
     \label{equ:single_level}\theta\leftarrow\arg\min_{\theta, \nu }L(\theta,\lambda,\nu) +\xi\underbrace{\sum_{i=1}^N D(\nu_i, \arg\max_{\pi_i} G_i(\pi_i;\theta))}_{\text{gap penalization for $\nu$}},
\end{align}
where $D(\cdot, \cdot)$ denotes a certain distance function, and $\xi>0$ is a hyper-parameter.
Note that here $\arg\max_{\pi_i} G_i(\pi_i;\theta)$ acts as a constant rather than a function of $\nu$ or $\theta$, making the gradient with respect to $\theta$ easier to compute.
Theoretically, as stated in \cref{thm:equivalence}, it can be proved that under mild \cref{assump:single_level}, the single-level optimization problem \eqref{equ:single_level} is nearly equivalent to the original bi-level optimization problem \eqref{equ:primal_domain_objective} in the primal domain update. The proofs are given in \cref{app:proof_equivalence}.
\begin{assumption}
\label{assump:single_level}
	Assume $\exists\mu\in\mathbb{R}_+$, such that $\forall \theta, i,\pi_i$, it holds that $	\|\nabla_{\pi_i}{G}_i(\pi_i;\theta)\|^2_2\ge{\mu}\big({G}_i(\nu_{i,\theta};\theta)-{G}_i(\pi_i;\theta)\big)$. Assume $L(\theta,\lambda,\nu)$ and ${G}_i(\pi_i,\theta)$ are both $Z$-Lipschitz smooth functions, where $Z>0$.
\end{assumption}


\begin{theorem}[Equivalence Between \eqref{equ:single_level} and \eqref{equ:primal_domain_objective}]
    \label{thm:equivalence}
    Under \cref{assump:single_level}, when $\xi\ge Z\sqrt{3/\mu\gamma}$,
the global and local solutions  to \eqref{equ:single_level}  are equivalent to the solutions to the $\gamma$-approximate problem of \eqref{equ:primal_domain_objective}.
\end{theorem}

However, the number of variables in $\nu$ is proportional to the number of agents, which can 
cause high computational complexity in solving \eqref{equ:single_level}. 
To address this issue, we raise the question of whether a unified solution, denoted as $x^*$, exists, that can effectively address all unilateral optimization problems. This would enable us to employ a single optimization variable, denoted as $\bar{\nu}$, that seeks to approximate $x^*$, thereby replacing $\nu$ in \eqref{equ:single_level} that has $N$ optimization variables, i.e.,
\begin{align}
     \label{equ:single_level_}\theta\leftarrow\arg\min_{\theta, \bar{\nu} }L(\theta,\lambda,\bar{\nu}) +\xi{ D(\bar{\nu}, x^*)}.
\end{align}
We refer to $\bar{\nu}$ as the \textbf{\emph{unified optimizer}}. In this way, we can make the computation complexity independent of the agent number. In fact, as described in \cref{sec:bpg_2}, we find that the POMG is a \textbf{\emph{permutation-equivariant} (PE)} game, which inherently makes the unified solution feasible. We derive the gradient of \eqref{equ:single_level_} in \cref{sec:bpg_3}.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
   \caption{Bi-level Policy Gradient (BPG) Framework}
   \label{algo:bi_level_policy_gradient}
\begin{algorithmic}
   \STATE {\bfseries Input:} Hyper-parameter $\xi$, step size $\alpha_1,\alpha_2$.
   \STATE {\bfseries Output:} Solution $\hat{\theta}^*$ to the NCB problem.
   \STATE{\bfseries Initializations:} Optimization variables $\theta^0$ and $\bar{\nu}^0$, iteration step $k\leftarrow 0$, sample ratio $\kappa_i=\frac{1}{N}$, $\forall i$.
   \REPEAT
   \STATE 1: Construct the weighted POMDP with $\kappa_i$ under $\theta^k$.
   \STATE 2: Learn the optimal policy as the unified solution with return $G_w^*(\theta^k)$ by standard policy gradient algorithm.
  \STATE 3: \textcolor{blue}{Dual domain update:} Calculate $\lambda^{k+1}_i, \forall i$ with \eqref{equ:dual_domain_update_final}.
  \STATE 4: \textcolor{blue}{Primal domain update:} Update $\theta^{k}$ and $\bar{\nu}^k$ to  $\theta^{k+1}$ and $\bar{\nu}^{k+1}$, respectively, with \eqref{equ:primal_domain_gradient_update}.
   \STATE 5: Update sample ratios $\kappa_i \leftarrow \frac{\lambda^{k+1}}{\bar{\lambda}^{k+1}}, \forall i$, where $\bar{\lambda}^{k+1}$ is the sum of all $\lambda_i^{k+1}$.
  \STATE 6: $k\leftarrow k+1$.
   \UNTIL{convergence}
   \STATE Let $\hat{\theta}^*\leftarrow\theta^k$ be the solution to the NCB problem.
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}
% \vspace{-2mm}

\subsection{Feasibility and Design of Unified Solution}
\label{sec:bpg_2}



\textbf{Feasibility of the Unified Solution.}  Under  \cref{assump:equ_mechanism}, the POMG is a PE game. 
%as stated in \cref{thm:pe}. 
See \cref{app:proof_pe} for the proof. 

\begin{theorem}[Permutation-Equivariant POMG]
    \label{thm:pe}
    Under \cref{assump:equ_mechanism}, the POMG is a permutation-equivariant game, i.e., for any permutation matrix $\rho_n$, the reward function and the transition rule satisfy: $r_t(\rho_n\mathbf{s}_t, \rho_n\mathbf{a}_t)=\rho_n r_t(\mathbf{s}_t,\mathbf{a}_t)$ and $P(\rho_n\mathbf{s}_{t+1}|\rho_n\mathbf{s}_t, \rho_n\mathbf{a}_t)=P(\mathbf{s}_{t+1}|\mathbf{s}_t,\mathbf{a}_t)$, respectively. 
%     \begin{align}
%         {r}_t(\rho_n\mathbf{s}_t, \rho_n\mathbf{a}_t)&=\rho_n r_t(\mathbf{s}_t,\mathbf{a}_t),\\
%         P(\rho_n\mathbf{s}_{t+1}|\rho_n\mathbf{s}_t, \rho_n\mathbf{a}_t)&=P(\mathbf{s}_{t+1}|\mathbf{s}_t,\mathbf{a}_t).
% \end{align}
\end{theorem}
\vspace{-1mm}
Specifically, we note that solving the $i$-th unilateral optimization problem is actually solving a \emph{partially observed Markov decision process} (POMDP) \cite{sutton2018reinforcement}, denoted as POMDP$_i$, with an initial distribution $\phi_i$. The permutation-equivariance property of POMG makes each POMDP$_i$ identical, except for the initial distributions. 
Hence, it is possible to learn a policy with the initial distribution information as input to address all unilateral optimization problems. This policy will act as the unified solution.
A more detailed explanation of identical POMDPs is given in \cref{app:pomdps}.

