\textbf{Unified Solution Design.}
To obtain the unified solution, we construct a \textbf{\emph{weighted POMDP} }that is identical to each POMDP but with a weighted initial distribution, $\sum_{i=1}^N\kappa_i\phi_i$, where $\kappa_i\in [0,1]$ is the sample ratio. Let the return of a certain policy $\pi_x$ in the weighted POMDP be $G_w(x;\theta)$.
Note that the policy $\pi_x$ should take the initial state of the weighted POMDP as input.
Then the unified solution is designed as $x^*=\arg\max_x G_w(x;\theta)$, which can be learned by a standard policy gradient algorithm \cite{sutton1999policy}.
For simplicity, we denote the  return of the unified solution as $G^*_w(\theta)\triangleq G_w(x^*;\theta)$.
Moreover, for the value of $\kappa_i$, 
a naive design is to let $\kappa_i=\frac{1}{N}$, leading to a uniform sampling. 
However, we recall that a larger Lagrange factor $\lambda_i$ indicates that agent $i$ can improve more unilaterally under current $\theta$. Hence, increasing the probability of sampling $\phi_i$ in the weighted POMDP will improve the expected return of agent $i$, which could help learn the unified solution. 
Motivated by this, we here design
$\kappa_i=\frac{\lambda_i}{\bar{\lambda}}$ to make it proportional to $\lambda_i$, where $\bar{\lambda}$ is the sum of all $\lambda_i$. 
%In this way, $\kappa_i$ is proportional to the violation degree of agent $i$ 
