\subsection{Policy Gradient of the Primal Domain Update}
\label{sec:bpg_3}
Equipped with the unified solution $x^*$,
% we can utilize one optimization variable, denoted as $\bar{\nu}$, that aims to approximate the unified solution, to replace $\nu$ in \eqref{equ:single_level} with $N$ optimization variables. 
% We refer to $\bar{\nu}$ as the \textbf{\emph{unified optimizer}},
% Then 
we implement the primal domain update \eqref{equ:single_level_} as:
\begin{align}
    \label{equ:simplified_single_level}\theta\leftarrow\arg\min_{\theta, \bar{\nu }}L(\theta,\lambda,\bar{\nu}) +\xi\underbrace{(G_w(\bar{\nu};\theta)-G_w^*(\theta))}_{\text{gap penalization for $\bar{\nu}$}},
\end{align}
where $D(\cdot,\cdot)$ is implemented as the gap between the return of $\bar{\nu}$ and the optimal return in the weighted POMDP. The gradient of \eqref{equ:simplified_single_level} can be expressed as \footnote{When $\bar{\lambda}=0$, the gradients  \eqref{equ:final_gradient} is degenerated to $\Delta\theta = -L_1(\theta)$ and $\Delta \bar{\nu}=0$. See \cref{app:lambda_zero} for an explanation. }:
\begin{align}
\label{equ:final_gradient}
      \begin{bmatrix}
	  	\Delta\theta\\
		\Delta\bar{\nu}
	\end{bmatrix}=
\begin{bmatrix}
		\xi L_w^*(\theta)+(1-\xi/\bar{\lambda})L_s(\theta, \bar{\nu})-L_1(\theta)\\
		(1-\xi/\bar{\lambda})L_g'(\theta,\bar{\nu})
	\end{bmatrix},
\end{align}
where $L_s$ is defined in \eqref{equ:original_gradient},  $L^*_w(\theta)\triangleq\nabla_\theta G_w(x;\theta)|_{x=x^*}$, and 
\begin{align}
    L_g'(\theta,\bar{\nu})=\sum_{i=1}^N\lambda_i\nabla_{\bar{\nu}}G_i(\bar{\nu};\theta).
\end{align}
The derivation process of \eqref{equ:final_gradient} is given in \cref{app:unified_gradient}.
Notably, compared to the competitive gradient $L_g$ in \eqref{equ:original_gradient}, $L_g'$ \textbf{avoids the complex term $\nabla_\theta\nu_{i,\theta}$}, making the gradient \eqref{equ:final_gradient}
straightforward to compute. 
In addition, it only involves two optimization variables, which are \textbf{independent of the number of agents}.
Based on the policy gradient theorem \cite{sutton1999policy}, we can derive the expressions of $L_1(\theta)$, $ L_s(\theta,\bar{\nu})$, $L_g'(\theta,\bar{\nu})$, and $L^*_w(\theta)$, respectively, which are all in the form of classical policy gradients \cite{sutton1999policy} and  straightforward to perform. 
The expressions of these terms are
summarized in \cref{table:policy_gradient_summary} in the appendix due to page limits, and detailed derivations are given in \cref{app:derivation_final_gradient}.  

