\subsection{Overall Bi-level Policy Gradient Framework}
\label{sec:overall_bpg}
We provide a summary of the BPG framework and make some practical modifications to enhance its stability and further decrease its spatial and computational complexities. Specifically, 
the BPG framework works in an iterative manner, alternating between a primal domain update and a dual domain update. During each iteration, we first construct the weighted POMDP with $\kappa_i$ under $\theta$ and learn its optimal policy $x^*$ as the unified solution by standard policy gradient algorithms. Then, we perform the dual domain update as: 
\begin{align}
\label{equ:dual_domain_update_final}
    \lambda_i\leftarrow [G_i(x^*;\theta)-G_i(\theta)-\epsilon]_+, \forall i,
\end{align}
Here we make two modifications compare to \eqref{equ:dual_update}. On the one hand, we use $x^*$ to replace $\nu_{i,\theta}$ to further decrease the computational complexity. On the other hand, we use the gradient $\nabla_{\lambda_i} L(\theta,\lambda, x^*)$ to update $\lambda_i$ directly, which can avoid maintaining a Lagrange factor $\lambda_i$ for each agent and help decrease the complexity of the space. 
Afterwards, we perform the primal domain update. Instead of completely solving \eqref{equ:simplified_single_level}, we update $\theta$ and $\bar{\nu}$ for only one step with the gradients in \eqref{equ:final_gradient} to facilitate the iteration process, i.e.,
\begin{align}
\label{equ:primal_domain_gradient_update}
    \theta \leftarrow \theta-\alpha_1\Delta\theta \quad\text{and}\quad
    \bar{\nu} \leftarrow \bar{\nu} -\alpha_2\Delta\bar{\nu},
\end{align}
where $\alpha_1,\alpha_2>0$ are step sizes.
Then we update the sample ratios $\kappa_i$ of the weighted POMDP with $\frac{\lambda_i}{\bar{\lambda_i}}$ and begin the next iteration until convergence.
The overall BPG framework is summarized in \cref{algo:bi_level_policy_gradient}. 


