    \field{abstract}{%
    Summary The paper deals with two main advantages in the analysis of slender
  elastic structures both achieved through the mixed (stress and displacement)
  format with respect to the more commonly used displacement one: (i) the
  smaller error in the extrapolations usually employed in the solution
  strategies of nonlinear problems and (ii) the lower polynomial dependence of
  the problem equations on the finite element degrees of freedom when solid
  finite elements are used. The smaller extrapolation error produces a lower
  number of iterations and larger step length in path-following analysis and a
  greater accuracy in Koiter asymptotic method. To focus on the origin of the
  phenomenon, the two formats are derived for the same finite element
  interpolation. The reduced polynomial dependence improves the Koiter
  asymptotic strategy in terms of both computational efficiency, accuracy and
  simplicity. Copyright {\copyright} 2016 John Wiley \& Sons, Ltd.%
    }
