    \label{fig_firstquadrant}
\end{figure}

Example~\ref{ex:3} showcases how we can split and use the $\bar{x}\in C$ condition to our benefit. This can be done on a broader scale. We can first calculate a CAD of $C$ and then apply QE to individual cells, $C_i$s, of the CAD. Calculating a decomposition of $C$ via CAD involves the original $n$ variables corresponding to the dimensions of the problem and is a much smaller question than calculating the whole normal cone at a fixed point which requires us to do QE on $2n+2$ variables, where the ordering of the variables are also restricted due to the quantified structure.

