Despite their success in training \glspl{snn}, \gls{bp}-based algorithms remain challenging to implement on neuromorphic hardware~\citep{neftciEventDrivenRandomBackPropagation2017,zenkeBrainInspiredLearningNeuromorphic2021,rennerBackpropagationAlgorithmImplemented2024}.
%journeHebbianDeepLearning2023
One key challenge is that neuromorphic hardware typically uses unidirectional synapses (like biological systems), requiring separate networks with distinct weights for the forward and backward passes~\citep{rennerBackpropagationAlgorithmImplemented2024}.
We refer to this setup as dual-network configuration, illustrated in Figure~\ref{chap:fbp:fig:dual-network}.
In this setup, only the forward weights are updated, based on neuron errors computed with the feedback weights.
To ensure correct credit assignment, the feedback weights must remain perfectly symmetric with the forward weights during training, which requires realignment after each iteration (i.e., each update of the forward weights).
This issue, known as the weight transport problem~\citep{grossbergCompetitiveLearningInteractive1987,akroutDeepLearningWeight2019,rennerBackpropagationAlgorithmImplemented2024}, or the weight symmetry requirement~\citep{liaoHowImportantWeight2016,kuninTwoRoutesScalable2020}, is both challenging and costly due to increased circuitry and energy costs associated with data movement~\citep{craftonLocalLearningRRAM2019,zenkeBrainInspiredLearningNeuromorphic2021}.

Several alternatives to \gls{bp} have been explored to eliminate or reduce the need for weight transport~\cite{kaiserSynapticPlasticityDynamics2020,zhangTuningConvolutionalSpiking2021,mirsadeghiSpikeTimeDisplacementBased2023,goupyNeuronalCompetitionGroups2024,bachoLowvarianceForwardGradients2024}.
Among them, feedback-driven methods~\citep{lillicrapRandomSynapticFeedback2016,noklandDirectFeedbackAlignment2016,xiaoBiologicallyPlausibleLearningAlgorithms2019,akroutDeepLearningWeight2019} are particularly attractive because they enable \gls{bp}-like training.
\gls{fa}~\citep{lillicrapRandomSynapticFeedback2016} eliminates weight transport by propagating errors through fixed, random feedback matrices instead of the exact transpose of the forward weights.
%Despite the lack of symmetry, learning remains possible as the forward weights gradually align with the feedback signal induced by the random feedback weights~\citep{cheonPretrainingRandomNoise2025}.
%refinettiAlignThenMemorise2021
%\gls{dfa}~\citep{noklandDirectFeedbackAlignment2016} extends \gls{fa} by directly transmitting errors from the output layer to each hidden layer, allowing layers to update their weights in parallel.
\gls{fa} and its variants have been widely studied for training shallow \glspl{snn}~\citep{neftciEventDrivenRandomBackPropagation2017,zhaoGLSNNMultiLayerSpiking2020,shresthaInHardwareLearningMultilayer2021,heSTSFSpikingTime2025}.
%leeSpikeTrainLevelDirect2020,shiDeepTempoHardwareFriendlyDirect2021
However, studies on \glspl{ann} have shown that it struggles to scale to deeper networks~\citep{bartunovAssessingScalabilityBiologicallyMotivated2018,moskovitzFeedbackAlignmentDeep2019,xiaoBiologicallyPlausibleLearningAlgorithms2019}, unless sign symmetry between forward and feedback weights is enforced, as in \gls{sfa}~\citep{liaoHowImportantWeight2016,moskovitzFeedbackAlignmentDeep2019}.
To date, \gls{sfa} has only been applied to \glspl{ann}.
Alternatively, \gls{ss}~\citep{xiaoBiologicallyPlausibleLearningAlgorithms2019} offers a relaxed form of weight symmetry, where the feedback weights are reduced to the sign of the forward weights.
This approach scales better to deeper \gls{ann} networks than \gls{fa}~\citep{xiaoBiologicallyPlausibleLearningAlgorithms2019}, but it still relies on sign transport to maintain sign symmetry, similar to \gls{sfa}.
%despite the feedback weights being restricted to binary values (i.e., \(-1\) and \(1\)).
%but still relies on sign transport similarly to \gls{sfa}.
Exploration of \gls{ss} in \glspl{snn} is limited; to our knowledge, only one study has used it, for continual learning~\citep{xiaoHebbianLearningBased2024}.
%Other efforts introduce mechanisms for learning feedback weights, such as weight synchronization via perturbations~\citep{kuninTwoRoutesScalable2020}, reciprocal learning using a modified Kolen-Pollack algorithm~\citep{akroutDeepLearningWeight2019}, or symmetric weight updates~\citep{rennerBackpropagationAlgorithmImplemented2024}.
%These methods require training both forward and feedback networks, which can become computationally and energy expensive~\citep{shibuyaFixedWeightDifferenceTarget2023}.
In summary, \gls{ss} and \gls{sfa} offer simple and relatively scalable alternatives to exact weight symmetry when relying on sign transport.
However, their application to deep \glspl{snn} remains largely unexplored.
In addition, the magnitude mismatch between forward and feedback weights inevitably introduces bias, distorting the direction of the gradients.
%leading to inaccurate gradient estimates
To better approximate the gradient directions of \gls{bp}, there is a need for methods that relax weight symmetry while minimizing this magnitude mismatch.
%To achieve learning closer to \gls{bp},

In this paper, we address weight symmetry in \gls{ttfs}-based deep \glspl{snn} under dual-network configurations.
We aim to improve training efficiency by reducing the frequency of weight transport and minimizing the synchronization overhead between the forward and feedback networks.
Our main contributions can be summarized as follows:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item We introduce \gls{fbp}, a \gls{bp}-based training algorithm that reduces the frequency of weight transport by updating forward weights using gradients computed from frozen feedback weights. At fixed intervals, the forward weights are transported to realign the feedback weights, minimizing magnitude mismatch and preserving gradient direction.
          %\item We introduce \gls{fbp}, a \gls{bp}-based training algorithm relaxing weight symmetry in dual-network configurations. \gls{fbp} updates forward weights by computing gradients using periodically frozen feedback weights, reducing the frequency of weight transport during training and thereby minimizing synchronization overhead between the forward and feedback networks.
    \item To further optimize transport efficiency, we propose a partial weight transport scheme where only a subset of the weights is transported at a time. We design three selection strategies of varying computational complexities.
    \item We benchmark \gls{sfa} and \gls{ss} for the first time in \gls{ttfs}-based deep \glspl{snn}. We show that \gls{fbp} outperforms these methods on image recognition tasks and achieves accuracy comparable to \gls{bp}. Also, we study the trade-off between transport efficiency and accuracy: with partial weight transport, \gls{fbp} lowers transport costs by \(1{,}000\times\) with an accuracy drop of only \(0.5\)~pp on CIFAR-10 and \(1.1\)~pp on CIFAR-100, or by up to \(10{,}000\times\) at the expense of moderated accuracy loss.
          %When combined with partial weight transport, it can reduce the accuracy loss or further lower transport costs by up to \(10{,}000\times\).
\end{enumerate}
This work provides insights to guide future efforts in designing neuromorphic hardware with efficient \gls{bp}-based on-chip learning.
The source code is publicly available at: \url{https://gitlab.univ-lille.fr/fox/fbp}.


\section{Preliminaries} \label{chap:fbp:sec:prelim}

\subsection{TTFS-Based Deep SNN Baseline}

Our work builds upon the \gls{ttfs}-based deep \gls{snn} introduced in~\citep{weiTemporalCodedSpikingNeural2023}, selected for its state-of-the-art performance and training efficiency.
This framework features \gls{ttfs} coding, single-spike \gls{relpsp} neurons, non-overlapping spike time windows, and event-driven \gls{bp} algorithm.
Full specifications and motivations are provided in Appendix~\ref{chap:fbp:sec:baseline}.

Training \gls{ttfs}-based \glspl{snn} using \gls{bp} involves computing gradients with respect to spike times:
\begin{equation}
    \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial W^l} =
    \underbrace{
        \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial t^L}
        \frac{\partial t^L}{\partial t^{L-1}}
        \cdots
        \frac{\partial t^{l+1}}{\partial t^{l}}
    }_{\delta^l}
    \frac{\partial t^{l}}{\partial W^{l}},
    \label{chap:fbp:eq:grad-dldw}
\end{equation}
where \(t^l\) are the spike times of layer \(l\), \(W^l\) are the weights of layer \(l\), \(L\) is the output layer, and \(\mathcal{L}\) is the loss.
We refer to \(\delta^l=\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial t^l}\) as the neuron errors, which is the gradient of the loss with respect to the spike times of neurons in layer \(l\).
%In the above equation, \(t\) and \(W\) are tensors.
The expressions for the partial derivatives in Equation~\ref{chap:fbp:eq:grad-dldw} depend on the specific \gls{snn} model used; full derivations for our baseline are included in Appendix~\ref{chap:fbp:sec:prelim-bp}.

\subsection{BP-Based Training in Dual-Network Configurations} \label{chap:fbp:sec:prelim-dualnetwork}
In a dual-network configuration (Figure~\ref{chap:fbp:fig:dual-network}), forward and backward passes are computed by two networks with distinct weights: a forward network with weights \(W\), and a feedback network with weights \(B\).
%The forward network propagates input spikes to the output and computes spike times.
%The feedback network backpropagates output errors to the input and computes neuron errors.
%These neuron errors act as feedback signals triggering weight updates only in the forward network.
%In a dual-network configuration (Figure~\ref{chap:fbp:fig:dual-network}), forward and backward passes are performed by separate networks with distinct weights \(W\) and \(B\), respectively.
%To ensure correct gradient propagation, feedback weights must remain symmetric to forward weights (\(B=W^\mathrm{T}\)), a constraint known as the weight symmetry requirement, or weight transport problem~\citep{grossbergCompetitiveLearningInteractive1987}.
Algorithm~\ref{chap:fbp:alg:bp-dualnetwork} provides a high-level overview of how training is carried out.
Note that the forward network shares spike times with the feedback network to compute neuron errors, while the feedback network shares neuron errors with the forward network to compute weight changes.
%, as shown in Figure~\ref{chap:fbp:fig:dual-network}.

The weight symmetry requirement for correct gradient propagation demands weight transport between forward and feedback weights.
This transport is performed by copying \(W\) to \(B\) after each iteration (i.e., after each update of \(W\)), implemented through the \textsc{WeightTransport} function in step~\ref{chap:fbp:alg:step-wt}.
Several approaches have been proposed to relax this constraint; below, we formally describe two main feedback-driven methods.

\begin{algorithm}
    \caption{BP training in a dual-network configuration}
    \small
    \begin{algorithmic}[1]
        \State FN: Forward Network; BN: Feedback Network
        \For{\textbf{each} iteration \(i\)}
        \For{\(l = 1\) \textbf{to} \(L\)}
        \State (FN) Compute spike times \(t^l\) with \(W^l\) \Comment{Eq~\ref{chap:fbp:eq:relpsp-t}}
        \State (FN) Send \(t^l\) to the corresponding feedback layer
        \EndFor
        \State Compute loss \(\mathcal{L}\) from \(t^L\) \Comment{Eq~\ref{chap:fbp:eq:ce-loss}}
        %\State Compute output neuron errors \(\delta^L\) from \(\mathcal{L}\)
        %\State Compute 
        %\State \textbf{Forward pass:} \(\gamma^{(l)} = f^{(l)}(\gamma^{(l-1)}, \mathbf{W}^{(l)})\)
        %\State \textbf{Loss computation:} \(\mathcal{L}(\gamma^{(L)}, \text{target})\)
        \For{\(l = L\) \textbf{to} \(1\)}
        \State (BN) Compute neuron errors \(\delta^l\) with \(B^l\) \Comment{Eq~\ref{chap:fbp:eq:grad-dtdt}}\label{chap:fbp:alg:step-db}
        \State (BN) Send \(\delta^l\) to the corresponding forward layer
        \State (FN) Compute \(\frac{\partial t^l}{\partial W^l}\) with \(W^l\) \Comment{Eq~\ref{chap:fbp:eq:grad-dtdw}}
        \State (FN) Update \(W^l\) with \(\delta^l\) and \(\frac{\partial t^l}{\partial W^l}\) \Comment{Eq~\ref{chap:fbp:eq:grad-dldw}}
        %\State Update weights \(W^l \leftarrow \mathbf{W}^{(l)} - \eta \nabla_{\mathbf{W}^{(l)}} \mathcal{L}\)
        \EndFor
        \State (FN) \textsc{WeightTransport}\(\left(W, B, i\right)\) \label{chap:fbp:alg:step-wt}
        %\State (FN) Optionally: copy \(W\) to \(B\)  \label{chap:fbp:alg:step-wt}
        \EndFor
    \end{algorithmic}
    \label{chap:fbp:alg:bp-dualnetwork}
\end{algorithm}

%The \gls{bp} algorithm described in Section~\ref{chap:fbp:sec:prelim-bp} requires access to the weight values to backpropagate the gradients from the output to the input layer.
%This setup introduces a critical constraint on the feedback network.
%Enforcing this symmetry requires transporting the weights from forward to feedback synapses after each iteration, resulting in significant data movement, higher energy costs, and increased latency~\citep{craftonLocalLearningRRAM2019,zenkeBrainInspiredLearningNeuromorphic2021}.

\paragraph{Feedback Alignment}
\gls{fa} eliminates weight symmetry by defining \(B\) as a fixed, random matrix.
To improve scalability to deeper networks, the \gls{sfa} variant preserves the sign of the forward weights in the feedback path, removing weight transport but introducing sign transport instead.
In this case, \(B = \hat{B} \circ \mathrm{sign}\left(W\right)\), where \(\hat{B}\) is initialized once and fixed throughout training, \(\mathrm{sign}\left(\cdot\right)\) is the element-wise sign function, and \(\circ\) is element-wise multiplication.
This approach can be implemented by modifying the \textsc{WeightTransport} function to update the signs of \(B\) to match those of \(W\).

\paragraph{Sign-Symmetry}
\gls{ss} reduces \(B\) to the sign of \(W\): \(B = \mathrm{sign}\left(W\right)\).
%, with \(\mathrm{sign}\left(\cdot\right)\) denoting the element-wise sign function.
Similar to \gls{sfa}, sign transport is required to maintain sign symmetry throughout training, which can be implemented by modifying the \textsc{WeightTransport} function as above.
% to update the signs of \(B\) to match those of \(W\).
Unlike \gls{sfa}, however, \gls{ss} eliminates stochasticity, since the feedback weights are restricted to either \(+1\) or \(-1\).

%In conclusion, both \gls{sfa} and \gls{ss} enable scalable training without exact weight symmetry, though their application to deep \glspl{snn} remains largely unexplored.
