Other approaches rely on mechanisms to learn the feedback weights rather than transporting the forward weights to them~\citep{kuninTwoRoutesScalable2020,akroutDeepLearningWeight2019,rennerBackpropagationAlgorithmImplemented2024}.
Similar to \gls{fbp}, these approaches require sharing neuron spike times and errors between networks.
%Them = after each iteration, us = after N iterations
However, they also double (at least) the number of weight updates per iteration, increasing both computational and energy costs~\citep{shibuyaFixedWeightDifferenceTarget2023,rennerBackpropagationAlgorithmImplemented2024}.
%In~\citep{rennerBackpropagationAlgorithmImplemented2024}, a symmetric learning rule is used to train feedback weights on the Loihi neuromorphic chip~\citep{daviesLoihiNeuromorphicManycore2018}, revealing that the on-chip learning engine accounts for most of the energy consumption (\(0.59\) mJ during training vs. \(0.02\) mJ during inference).
%Yet, the specific energy impact of feedback updates remains unknown.
At this stage, it is unclear which approach is best suited for \gls{bp}-based training on neuromorphic hardware.
The key challenge of \gls{fbp} is to find the optimal trade-off between transport frequency and accuracy that makes its overhead practical for hardware implementation.
This work does not claim our method to be the optimal solution, but rather seeks to provide insights for hardware design.

Theoretically, \gls{fbp} is agnostic to network architecture and \gls{bp} algorithm.
Regarding architecture, it can be applied to any parameterized architecture requiring forward and feedback paths.
Our method can be extended to other trainable parameters, such as biases or batch normalization parameters, as the transport mechanism applies identically.
%However, layers with a large number of parameters may be less suited to partial weight transport, as transporting only a small fraction of weights could result in insufficient correction of gradient bias.
Regarding training algorithms, \gls{fbp} does not change the form of the update equations but computes neuron errors using distinct weights.
It is therefore compatible, in principle, with a wide range of \gls{bp}-based methods for \glspl{snn}, including \gls{bptt}, and, more generally, with traditional \gls{bp} for \glspl{ann}.
The only requirement is that gradients must be backpropagated through the exact reverse circuit of the forward pass.
%This requirement excludes methods like \gls{dfa}~\citep{noklandDirectFeedbackAlignment2016} or direct random target projection~\citep{frenkelLearningFeedbackFixed2021}, which employ different feedback circuits.
%which bypass the feedback path symmetry and do not rely on gradients propagated through the exact reverse circuit.
However, freezing the feedback weights induces biased neuron errors.
The more updates are performed with these biased errors, the more the learning trajectory may diverge.
Thus, \gls{fbp} may not adapt equally well to all algorithms, particularly those with dense updates, such as \gls{bptt}.
%This can be particularly problematic for algorithms with dense or frequent updates, such as \gls{bptt}.
Our experiment in Appendix~\ref{chap:fbp:sec:appendix:results:st}, where our freezing mechanism was applied to \gls{ss}, suggested that \gls{fbp} may generalize to other \gls{bp}-based methods.
Yet, further research is needed to determine under which conditions \gls{fbp} remains effective.

%The concept of freezing feedback weights for \(\Phi\) iterations in \gls{fbp} differs from the traditional notion of batch in gradient descent.
%In \gls{fbp}, forward weights are updated in the same way as standard gradient descent (i.e., per batch) and can even be adjusted online, similar to stochastic gradient descent.
%However, \gls{fbp} introduces a second notion of batch with \(\Phi\), referring to the number of iterations over which the feedback weights remain frozen.
%Hence, \gls{fbp} can be interpreted as explicitly computing gradients using a stale version of the forward weights.
\gls{fbp} computes gradients using a stale version of the forward weights.
This approach raises similarities with methods employing delayed gradients~\citep{zhengAsynchronousStochasticGradient2017,zhuangFullyDecoupledNeural2022}, often encountered in asynchronous learning scenarios where workers compute gradients based on outdated model weights.
However, \gls{fbp} differs by separating forward and feedback weights (dual-network configuration), and by explicitly freezing the feedback weights for a given number of iterations (typically higher than in asynchronous learning).
Still, these characteristics make \gls{fbp} conceptually relevant to distributed~\citep{zhengAsynchronousStochasticGradient2017} and federated~\citep{zhuDelayedGradientAveraging2021} learning, which could inspire future work to draw from these domains, for example to mitigate the bias introduced by stale gradients~\citep{zhengAsynchronousStochasticGradient2017,zhuangFullyDecoupledNeural2022}.
