\subsection{Neural Coding} \label{chap:fbp:sec:prelim-coding}
%Since spiking neurons communicate through spikes, the input must be encoded before being processed by the \gls{snn}.
%There are two primary coding schemes studied in the literature~\citep{augeSurveyEncodingTechniques2021,guoNeuralCodingSpiking2021}: rate %coding and temporal coding.
%In rate coding, the intensity of the input is encoded into the firing frequency of neurons.
%Conversely, temporal coding encodes the intensity of the input into the precise timing of individual spikes.
%Compared to rate coding, temporal coding offers higher efficiency in terms of spike counts \TODO{finish}.
%Compared to rate coding, temporal coding offers faster information transfer, lower computational cost, and improved energy efficiency~\citep{thorpeSpikeBasedStrategiesRapid2001,rullenRateCodingTemporal2001,parkT2FSNNDeepSpiking2020,guoNeuralCodingSpiking2021}.
%rate coding neglects the temporal information carried by spikes
For the input encoding layer, we use the \gls{ttfs} temporal coding scheme, also referred to as first-spike or latency coding~\citep{bonillaAnalyzingTimetoFirstSpikeCoding2022}.
This scheme converts each input value \(x \in \left[0,1\right]\) to a single spike time $t\left(x\right)$:
\begin{equation}
    t\left(x\right)= T_w \cdot \left(1 - x\right),
    \label{chap:fbp:eq:ttfs}
\end{equation}
where \(T_w\) is the length of the permissible spike time window (set to \(1\) in this work).
Consequently, the intensity of the value is encoded through latency: higher values correspond to lower latencies, and vice versa.
%Spike timestamps are represented by floating-point values to align with spike-based communication in event-driven neuromorphic hardware.


\subsection{Neuron Model} \label{chap:fbp:sec:prelim-relpsp}
To simulate the dynamics of spiking neurons, we adopt the \gls{relpsp} neuron model~\citep{zhangRectifiedLinearPostsynaptic2022}, which can be viewed as an \gls{if} model with linear post-synaptic potential function.
% with a rectified linear synaptic current kernel function to shape the \gls{psp} of the neurons.
Unlike simple \gls{if} neurons with instantaneous synapses~\citep{kheradpishehTemporalBackpropagationSpiking2020}, the membrane potential of \gls{relpsp} neurons increases continuously and linearly over time after receiving an input spike, even in the absence of subsequent spikes.
% the membrane potential is described by the integration of kernels reflecting the incoming currents at the synapse
% continuous PSP
The membrane potential \(V^l_j\) of neuron \(n^l_j\) in layer $l$ is expressed as:
\begin{equation}
    V^l_j\left(t\right) = \sum_{i=1}^{N^{l-1}} W^l_{ij} \cdot \epsilon\left(t - t^{l-1}_i\right),
    \label{chap:fbp:eq:relpsp-v}
\end{equation}
where \(t\) is the current time, \(N^{l-1}\) is the number of neurons in layer \(l-1\), \(t^{l-1}_i\) is the spike time of neuron \(n^{l-1}_i\), \(W^l_{ij}\) is the weight of the synapse from \(n^{l-1}_i\) to \(n^l_j\), and \(\epsilon\left(\cdot\right)\) is the kernel function, defined as:
\begin{equation}
    \epsilon\left(t - t^{l-1}_i\right) = \begin{cases}
        t - t^{l-1}_i & \text{if } t > t^{l-1}_i \\
        0             & \text{o.w. }
    \end{cases}.
    \label{chap:fbp:eq:relpsp-eps}
\end{equation}
When \(V^l_j\) reaches a threshold \(\theta\) at time \(t\) (i.e., \(V^l_j\left(t\right) \geq \theta\)), \(n^l_j\) emits a spike (\(t^l_j = t\)), resets its membrane potential to zero, and remain deactivated until the next sample is shown.
Consequently, aligned with \gls{ttfs} coding, \gls{relpsp} neurons can fire at most once per sample and encode the intensity of their activation through spike timing: neurons firing first are the most strongly activated.

To define the thresholds of the neurons, we employ \gls{dft}~\citep{weiTemporalCodedSpikingNeural2023}.
In this method, the threshold \(\theta^l\) of layer \(l\) is modeled as a layer-dependent, time-varying function, linearly decreasing over time:
\begin{equation}
    \theta^l\left(t\right) = \begin{cases}
        T_w \cdot\left(l+1\right) - t, & \text{if } T_w \cdot l \leq t \leq T_w \cdot \left(l+1\right) \\
        +\infty                        & \text{o.w. }
    \end{cases}.
    \label{chap:fbp:eq:relpsp-dft}
\end{equation}
The encoding layer described in Appendix~\ref{chap:fbp:sec:prelim-coding} is regarded as the \(0\)-th layer.
With this method, neurons in the \(l\)-th layer can only fire within the spike time window \(\left[T_w \cdot l, T_w \cdot \left(l+1\right) \right]\), ensuring that spikes do not overlap across layers.
% layer-dependent firing feature.
% firing threshold of the neurons is computed dynamically

Based on Equation~\ref{chap:fbp:eq:relpsp-v} and Equation~\ref{chap:fbp:eq:relpsp-dft}, the spike time of the neurons has a closed-form solution:
\begin{equation}
    t_j^l =
    \begin{cases}
        T_w \cdot l                                                                                                      & \text{if } V_j^l \left(T_w \cdot l\right) \geq \theta^l \left(T_w \cdot l\right) \\
        \frac{T_w \cdot \left(l+1\right) + \sum_{i \in C^l_j} W_{ij}^l \cdot t_i^{l-1}}{1 + \sum_{i \in C^l_j} W_{ij}^l} & \text{if } T_w \cdot l < t_j^l \leq T_w \cdot \left(l+1\right)                   \\
        +\infty                                                                                                          & \text{o.w. }
    \end{cases},
    \label{chap:fbp:eq:relpsp-t}
\end{equation}
where \(C^l_j\) is the causal set\footnote{Assuming an output neuron \(n_j\) fires at \(t_j\), the causal set \(C_j = \{n_i : t_i < t_j\}\) is the subset of input neurons that fire before \(n_j\).} of neuron \(n^l_j\).


\subsection{Event-Driven BP} \label{chap:fbp:sec:prelim-bp}
The \gls{relpsp} neuron model described in Appendix~\ref{chap:fbp:sec:prelim-relpsp} enables exact gradient computation with respect to spike times~\cite{zhangRectifiedLinearPostsynaptic2022,stanojevicHighPerformanceDeepSpiking2024}.
Hence, event-driven \gls{bp} can be employed to train the \gls{snn}~\cite{weiTemporalCodedSpikingNeural2023}.
Since neurons encode the intensity of their activation through spike timing, we aim for the target neuron to fire first, while all others fire as late as possible.
To achieve this, we apply a cross-entropy loss to the negated spike times of the output layer \(L\):
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}(t^L, d) = -\log \frac{\exp\left(-t^L_d\right)}{\sum_{i=1}^M \exp\left(-t^L_i\right)},
    \label{chap:fbp:eq:ce-loss}
\end{equation}
where \(\mathcal{L}\) is the loss function, \(M\) is the number of output neurons (\(M\) is equal to the number of classes), and \(d\) is the target class index (ground-truth label).

We compute the derivative of the loss with respect to the weights following Equation~\ref{chap:fbp:eq:grad-dldw}.
The gradient \(\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial t^L}\) is obtained directly from the loss function in Equation~\ref{chap:fbp:eq:ce-loss}.
\gls{bp} then proceeds by iteratively computing, based on Equation~\ref{chap:fbp:eq:relpsp-t}, the derivatives at layer \(l\) of the output spike time \(t^l_j\) with respect to the weight \(W^l_{ij}\) and the input spike times \(t^{l-1}_i\):
%spike times with respect to preceding layer inputs and weights.
%Specifically, for a given layer \(l\), the gradients of  are given by:
%
%\begin{equation}
\begin{align}
    \frac{\partial t^l_j}{\partial W^l_{ij}}  & =
    \begin{cases}
        \frac{t^{l-1}_i - t^l_j}{1 + \sum_{i \in C^l_j} W^l_{ij}} & \text{if } T_w \cdot l < t^l_j < T_w \cdot \left(l+1\right) \\
        0                                                         & \text{o.w. }
    \end{cases},
    \label{chap:fbp:eq:grad-dtdw}                 \\
    %\end{equation}
    %\begin{equation}
    \frac{\partial t^l_j}{\partial t^{l-1}_i} & =
    \begin{cases}
        \frac{W^l_{ij}}{1 + \sum_{i \in C^l_j} W^l_{ij}} & \text{if } T_w \cdot l < t^l_j < T_w \cdot \left(l+1\right) \\
        0                                                & \text{o.w. }
    \end{cases}.
    \label{chap:fbp:eq:grad-dtdt}
\end{align}
%\end{equation}

\subsection{Motivations for the Baseline} \label{chap:fbp:sec:prelim-motivations}
\gls{ttfs} coding and the \gls{relpsp} model impose a single-spike constraint, effectively reducing the number of generated spikes.
This inherent spike sparsity makes the \gls{snn} particularly attractive for ultra-low-power applications.
Beyond efficiency, the \gls{relpsp} model enables exact gradient computation with respect to spike times~\citep{zhangRectifiedLinearPostsynaptic2022,stanojevicHighPerformanceDeepSpiking2024}, allowing direct application of event-driven \gls{bp}.
However, only a few \gls{ttfs}-based methods have scaled effectively to deep networks~\citep{parkTrainingEnergyEfficientDeep2021,zhouTemporalCodedDeepSpiking2021,weiTemporalCodedSpikingNeural2023,stanojevicHighPerformanceDeepSpiking2024}.
The key components we identify for successful scalability are:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*), leftmargin=*]
    \item \textit{Controlled sparsity}---Excessive sparsity must be avoided to ensure spike propagation in deeper layers and to prevent the dead neuron problem~\citep{eshraghianTrainingSpikingNeural2023} during \gls{bp}. The \gls{relpsp} model addresses this issue by enabling membrane potentials to increase continuously over time, even in the absence of further input spikes~\citep{zhangRectifiedLinearPostsynaptic2022}. The \gls{dft} mechanism further promotes spike generation by progressively lowering thresholds over the spike time window of the neurons~\citep{weiTemporalCodedSpikingNeural2023}. % which also reduces hyperparameter tuning
    \item \textit{Simulation and training speed}---The analytical expression for spike times (cf. Equation~\ref{chap:fbp:eq:relpsp-t}) enables GPU-based training of the \gls{snn} in a manner analogous to an \gls{ann}, without requiring simulation over multiple time steps. Additionally, applying layer-dependent firing constraints, where neurons in layer \(l\) cannot fire before neurons in layer \(l-1\), eliminates the need to explicitly compute the causal set~\citep{weiTemporalCodedSpikingNeural2023,stanojevicHighPerformanceDeepSpiking2024}, which is computationally expensive.
    \item \textit{Parameter initialization}---Training deeper networks is more challenging, particularly due to parameter initialization. While \gls{ann} pretraining has been proposed as a solution~\citep{weiTemporalCodedSpikingNeural2023,stanojevicHighPerformanceDeepSpiking2024}, we deliberately avoid it to maintain a fully spike-based training pipeline. This choice limits our ability to scale to deeper architectures such as VGG-16~\citep{weiTemporalCodedSpikingNeural2023,stanojevicHighPerformanceDeepSpiking2024}.
\end{enumerate}


