Here, we evaluate the relevance of our freezing mechanism when applied to \gls{ss}.
%, and compare its performance to \gls{fbp}.
We refer to this variant as \textsc{fSS} (frozen \gls{ss}).
In this case, weight signs are transported after \(\Phi\) iterations instead of every iteration, reducing the frequency of sign transport.
Figure~\ref{chap:fbp:fig:impact-phi}  shows that \textsc{fSS} successfully supports training with our frozen feedback weights, suggesting that our method may generalize beyond a specific \gls{bp}-based algorithm.
However, \gls{fbp} outperforms it across all datasets.
