\section{Conclusion}
This work introduces \emph{Heterogeneous Dynamic Logic}, a compositional framework for the verification of heterogeneous programs.
Similarly to how Satisfiability Modulo Theories modularly combines data logics to construct more expressive combined first-order theories, Heterogeneous Dynamic Logic modularly combines program logics to construct more expressive, combined heterogeneous program logics.
The resulting heterogeneous dynamic theories do not only enable us to \emph{specify} properties that would otherwise be hard to formalize (see \Cref{subsubsec:case-studies:controller:example}), but they also provide a proof calculus to decompose specified heterogeneous properties in a manner that is compatible with existing homogeneous verification infrastructure.
\Cref{sec:rel-complete} shows under reasonable assumptions that heterogeneous proof calculi inherit not just rules and axioms but also relative completeness.
This ensures that verifying heterogeneous programs is no harder than verifying properties about the homogeneous programs and their shared first-order structure (i.e., their data logic).
The proof theory of heterogeneous systems provided in this work will not only enable the combination of existing, dynamic logic-based verification methodologies, but also serves as a vehicle for future dynamic logic-based verification techniques, which will be able to seamlessly integrate with the existing ecosystem by using HDL.



