\section{Proof of Proposition 1 (Stochastic DP Existence)}
\pdfbookmark[2]{Proof of Proposition 1}{sec:appendix}
\noindent\textbf{Proposition:}  
Let the state spaces $\Theta\subseteq[0,1]$, $L\subseteq[0,L_{\max}]$, and $\Omega\subseteq[0,1-\alpha_B]$ be compact.  Assume:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\delta\in(0,1)$;
  \item $U_S(S,p,m)$ is bounded and jointly continuous in $(p,m,S)$;
  \item $f(S,p,m;\varepsilon,\nu)$ is continuous in $(\varepsilon,\nu)$, so that $\mathrm{E}[V(\cdot)]$ is well-defined;
  \item the local best response $c^*(p,m)$ is continuous in $(p,m)$ and truncated to $[c_{\min},1]$.
\end{itemize}
Then there exists at least one Markov-perfect strategy $\{p^*(S),m^*(S)\}$ and value function $V^*(S)$ satisfying the stochastic Bellman equation
\begin{equation*}
V(S)
= \max_{p,m}\,\mathrm{E}\bigl[\,U_S(S;p,m)\;+\;\delta\,V\bigl(f(S,p,m;\varepsilon,\nu)\bigr)\bigr],
\end{equation*}
which together with $c^*(p,m)$ form a dynamic Stackelberg equilibrium.

\noindent\textbf{Proof:}  
Let 
\[
  \mathscr V=\bigl\{V:\mathscr S\to\mathbb R \mid V\text{ bounded, continuous}\bigr\},
  \quad \|\!V\!\|_\infty=\sup_{S\in\mathscr S}|V(S)|,
  \quad \mathscr S=\Theta\times L\times\Omega.
\]
Define the Bellman operator $\mathcal T:\mathscr V\to\mathscr V$ by
\[
  (\mathcal T V)(S)
  = \max_{p,m}\,\mathrm{E}\bigl[\,U_S(S;p,m)\;+\;\delta\,V\bigl(f(S,p,m;\varepsilon,\nu)\bigr)\bigr].
\]
For any $V_1,V_2\in\mathscr V$ and all $S\in\mathscr S$,
\begin{align*}
  \bigl|(\mathcal T V_1)(S)-(\mathcal T V_2)(S)\bigr|
  &\le \max_{p,m}\mathrm{E}\Bigl[\delta\bigl|V_1(f(S,p,m;\cdot)) - V_2(f(S,p,m;\cdot))\bigr|\Bigr]\\
  &\le \delta\,\|V_1 - V_2\|_\infty.
\end{align*}
Since $\delta<1$, $\mathcal T$ is a contraction on the complete metric space $(\mathscr V,\|\cdot\|_\infty)$, so by Banach’s fixed‐point theorem there is a unique $V^*\in\mathscr V$ with $\mathcal T V^*=V^*$.  Then for each $S\in\mathscr S$ define measurable selectors
\[
  (p^*(S),m^*(S))
  = \arg\max_{p,m}\,\mathrm{E}\bigl[\,U_S(S;p,m)\;+\;\delta\,V^*(f(S,p,m;\varepsilon,\nu))\bigr].
\]
Continuity and compactness ensure the maximum is attained, and measurable‐selection arguments yield Borel‐measurable strategies.  Together with $c^*(p,m)$ these constitute the desired equilibrium.

\noindent Finally, verify the Stackelberg conditions:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Local Government Optimality}:  By Lemma 1,
  \[
    \frac{\partial}{\partial c}\Bigl[\alpha\theta - \tfrac12\kappa c^2 - \beta(c-p)^2\Bigr]
    = -\kappa c - 2\beta(c-p) = 0
    \;\;\Longrightarrow\;\;
    c^* = \frac{\beta p}{\kappa + \beta}.
  \]
  \item \emph{Central Government Optimality}:  By construction, $(p^*,m^*)$ maximizes the Bellman operator.
  \item \emph{Belief Consistency}:  Updates
  \[
    \theta_{t+1} = \theta_t + \eta\,(c_t p_t - \theta_t) + \varepsilon_t
  \]
  follow Bayesian learning and remain consistent.
\end{itemize}
Some further technical points:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Continuity Preservation}:  Maximum exists by continuity of $U_S$ and compactness of actions.
  \item \emph{Measurable Selection}:  Borel‐measurable selectors exist by standard theorems.
  \item \emph{Admissible Strategies}:  Truncation $c^*\in[c_{\min},1]$ ensures feasibility.
\end{itemize}
\qed{Q.E.D.}



\section{Constant Belief Justification}
\pdfbookmark[2]{Constant Belief}{sec:appendix}

In our model, Skeptics are assumed to form a constant belief about government policy credibility, denoted by $\theta$. We specify that $\theta$ can be taken as the ``historical average'' of credibility. Below we justify why this choice based on the affine dependence on $\theta$.

In the aggregate investment function, $\theta$ enters linearly as part of the Skeptics' investment term. Only the product $\omega_tk_S\theta$ appears for the Skeptics, with no nonlinear or higher-order terms in $\theta$. Hence, any constant shift of $\theta$ by a fixed amount would merely translate the level of that term.

All model equations and equilibrium conditions depend on $\theta$ only in this linear fashion. Constant shift does not alter any dynamic feedback or best-response conditions for other agents, because those rely on differences or linear combinations of credibility. Consequently, the model's outcomes are isomorphic.

Since any constant $\theta$ is effectively just a baseline level around which the Skeptics do not update, it is convenient to interpret $\theta$ as the historical average of credibility. The linearity implies that no generality is lost by doing so.
