\section{Numerical experiments}\label{sec:tests}
In this section we propose some numerical tests to show the performances of the proposed methods in terms of accuracy and efficiency. In all tests, the two-step second-order IMEX 2SBDF with step $\Delta t$ is initialized by performing $4/\Delta t$ steps of the first-order IMEX Euler with time step $(\Delta t/2)^2$ to have an accurate approximate solution at time $t=\Delta t$. We compare the proposed IMEX schemes with the numerical method proposed in \cite{GaoFD}, that combines a standard second-order finite difference approximation in space with the second-order accurate exponential Rosenbrock-Euler method in time for the stiff equation \eqref{AC} and with the implicit midpoint rule for the time integration of \eqref{CH}. The two equations are suitably decoupled, by using an explicit second-order approximation for $c$ in \eqref{AC}. This scheme is implemented in \textsc{Matlab} as in \cite{Conte} and the function $\varphi_1$ in exponential Rosenbrock-Euler method is calculated on matrix arguments by the algorithm \texttt{expmvp.m} in \cite{Niesen}.

All tests have been performed in \textsc{Matlab} R2024b on a 64-bit Windows desktop computer with a 2.10 GHz 12th Gen. Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-12700 Processor and 16.0 GB Memory. 
\subsection{Pencil electrode test}
As a first benchmark problem we consider a variant of the pencil electrode test in \cite{Ernst,GaoFD,GaoFE,mai}, already proposed in \cite{Conte}. The specimen is a 300 $\mu$m long and 25 $\mu$m wide metal wire mounted on an epoxy coating which leaves only the two ends of the wire exposed to an electrolyte solution.

This setting is modeled by system \eqref{AC}--\eqref{hg} on a rectangular domain $\Omega=[0,25]\times[0,300]$ with homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions at the wire ends,
$$\phi(x,0,t)=c(x,0,t)=\phi(x,300,t)=c(x,300,t)=0,$$
homogeneous Neumann boundary conditions on the rest of the boundary,
$$\frac{\partial}{\partial n}\phi(0,y,t)=\frac{\partial}{\partial n}c(0,y,t)=\frac{\partial}{\partial n}\phi(25,y,t)=\frac{\partial}{\partial n}c(25,y,t)=0,$$
and the constant initial condition
$$\phi(x,y,0)=c(x,y,0)=1$$
in the interior of $\Omega$. As in \cite{Conte} we integrate till the final time $T=225$s. 

To measure the accuracy of the different methods we compute a reference solution $(\phi_{\text{ref}},c_{\text{ref}})$, by using the method in \cite{GaoFD,Conte} on a fine grid with steps
$$\Delta x=\Delta y=0.5\mu\text{m},\qquad \Delta t=10^{-5}\text{s}$$
and tolerance $10^{-8}$ for the computation of the matrix function $\varphi_1$. The errors in the solution $(\phi,c)$ of the different methods is computed at the final time as 
$$\text{Err}_\phi=\frac{\|\phi-\phi_{\text{ref}}\|}{\|\phi_{\text{ref}}\|},\qquad \text{Err}_c=\frac{\|c-c_{\text{ref}}\|}{\|c_{\text{ref}}\|},$$
where $\|\cdot\|$ is the Euclidean vector norm.

We first solve the problem by IMEX Euler method \eqref{finAC}--\eqref{finCH} with
$$
\Delta x=\Delta y=1\mu\text{m},\qquad \Delta t=10^{-3}\text{s},\qquad w=4.43\cdot 10^8.
$$
The value of $w$ has been obtained by suitably estimating (a priori) the module of the largest eigenvalue of the Jacobian matrix of $F_1(\boldsymbol{\phi},\mathbf{c})$ on the solution along the full integration time and space domain. With this value of $w$ condition \eqref{fov} with \eqref{AwBw2} is satisfied at each time step.
