\begin{Proposition} \label{prop: def1}
    Assume the valuations of the original apartments are identical for the agents.
    Then, there exists an allocation $A$ of the new apartments $M$ and a balanced payment vector $\mathbf{p}$ such that $(A, \mathbf{p})$ is DEF. Moreover, $A$ and $\mathbf{p}$ can be found in polynomial time.
\end{Proposition}
\begin{proof}
   Consider a cycle $C$. The cost of $C$ in $G_O$ is $0$. Then, to compute an DEF-able allocation of the new apartments, the cost of each cycle in the envy graph corresponding to the new allocation should be less than $0$. This can be done easily by finding the 
   maximum-value 
   matching between the agents and the new apartments in polynomial time.

   Computing the payments can be done by finding the subsidies as shown by \citet{halpern2019fair}, that is, the subsidy of each agent is the maximum cost path starting at that agent.
   To obtain a balanced payment vector, we reduce the average payment from each of the subsidies; this has no effect on the envy.
\end{proof}

\begin{Proposition} \label{prop: def2}
    Let $O$ represent the allocation of the original apartments.
    Assume that every cycle in $G_O$, the envy graph corresponding to the original apartments, has a non-negative cost.
    Then, there exists an allocation $A$ of the new apartments $M$ and a balanced payment vector $\mathbf{p}$ such that $(A, \mathbf{p})$ is DEF. Moreover, $A$ and $\mathbf{p}$ can be found in polynomial time.
\end{Proposition}
\begin{proof}
According to \citet{halpern2019fair}, an allocation $A$ is EF-able if and only if $A$ maximizes utilitarian welfare across all possible reassignments of bundles to agents, and if and only if the envy graph corresponding to $A$ has no positive-cost cycles.

Let $A$ denote the allocation that maximizes the total value of the new apartments (i.e., a maximum-value matching
between agents and the new apartments). That is, $A$ is EF-able and has no positive-cost cycles in $G_A$.

Now, consider a cycle $C$. From condition (c) of Theorem 1 in \citet{halpern2019fair}, $cost_A(C) \leq 0$. Moreover, by assumption, $cost_O(C) \geq 0$. Consequently, $cost_{A,O}(C) = cost_A(C) - cost_O(C) \leq 0$.
Thus, by condition \ref{condition b of theorem thm:efable--iff-cost-cycles} of \Cref{thm:efable--iff-cost-cycles}, $A$ is DEF-able.

The payment vector can be computed using the same method outlined in the proof of \cref{prop: def1}.
%by finding the subsidies as shown by \citet{halpern2019fair}, that is, the subsidy of each agent is the maximum cost path starting at that agent.
 %  To obtain a balanced payment vector, we reduce the average payment from each of the subsidies; this has no effect on the envy.
\end{proof}


\begin{Proposition}
\label{prop:no-defable}
    There are allocation of originals apartments $O$ and a set of new apartments $M$ such that, for every allocations of one apartment per agent, the resulting allocation is not DEF-able.
\end{Proposition}
   \begin{proof}
   \begin{Example} \label{example: no def}
       Consider the following example with 3 agents.
The values of the original apartments are
\[
\begin{bmatrix}
   & o_1 & o_2 &o_3 \\
  i_1 & z + \epsilon & z & z\\
  i_2 & y & y + \epsilon & y \\
  i_3 & x & x & x + \epsilon \\
\end{bmatrix}.
\]
The values for the new apartments are
\[
\begin{bmatrix}
   & a_1 & a_2 &a_3 \\
  i_1 & V_1 & V_1 & V_1\\
  i_2 & V_2 & V_2 & V_2 \\
  i_3 & V_3 & V_3 & V_3 \\
\end{bmatrix}.
\]
when $x,y,z,\epsilon,V_1, V_2, V_3 \in \mathbb{N}$.
Notice that the cost of the cycle $i_1, i_2, i_3$ in $G_O$ is $-3\epsilon$. For each allocation $A$ of the new apartments, the cost of the cycle $i_1, i_2, i_3$ in $G_A$ is $0$. When considering the original apartments, the cost of the cycle $i_1, i_2, i_3$ in $G_{A,O}$ is $3\epsilon > 0$. Therefore, there is no DEF allocation possible for this example.
   \end{Example}
       Specific instances of \Cref{example: no def} include:
       \begin{enumerate}
           \item The scenario where all new apartments are valued equally, i.e., $V_1 = V_2 = V_3$.
           \item The scenario where each agent perceives no personal improvement but believes that others have benefited.
           \begin{Example} \label{example:no-ef-allocation}
           Consider the same valuations of the old apartments as in \Cref{example: no def}.
           The values for the new apartments are
