The example above is important because it shows that no matter how we allow the agents to pick the items, the resulting allocation will not be DEF-able. The DEF-able allocation forces agents 1 and 2 to receive items they value less in order to increase the negative envy of agent 3 towards agent 1.

Now, we demonstrate how to construct a payment vector for a given allocation that minimizes the maximum envy, while also determining the value of this maximum envy. This is motivated by the fact that a DEF-able allocation does not always exist (as shown in \Cref{prop:no-defable}).
We can do this by solving a linear program, but there is also a combinatorial algorithm.
\begin{Definition}
Given a directed graph $G$ with edge costs, 
The \emph{mean cost} of a path is the total cost of the path divided by the number of edges in it.

The \emph{maximum average cycle cost (MACC)} of $G$ is the maximum, over all directed cycles $C$ in $G$, of the mean cost of $C$.
\end{Definition}
In the literature, the MACC of $G$ is  also known as the \emph{maximum mean cycle weight} \cite{wikipedia-minimum-weight-cycle}.

%The proofs in this section for the difference and ratio models differs in the original graph: $G_{A,O}$ for the difference model and $G_{A,w}$ for the ratio model. For convenience, let us denote the original graph as $G$.
%Given that a WEF-able allocation does not always exist (see \Cref{prop: not WEF-able}), suppose we are provided with a fixed allocation and aim to find a payment vector that \emph{minimizes the maximum envy}.
\begin{Lemma} \label{lemm: envy bound mean cycle}
For a given allocation $A$, there exists a payment vector $\mathbf{p}$ such that the envy between any two agents is bounded by the maximum mean cycle cost of $G_{A,O}$. Specifically, for all $i,j \in N$, the following holds:
$$
(v_i(A_j) - v_i(o_j) + p_j) - \left(v_i(A_i) - v_i(o_i) + p_i \right) \leq c,$$ 
where $c := MACC(G_{A,O})$.
\end{Lemma}

\begin{proof} 
Let $C$ be the maximum mean cycle in $G_{A,O}$.
meaning that for any other cycle $C'$, it holds that $c = \frac{cost_A(C)}{|C|} \geq \frac{cost_A(C')}{|C'|}$.

Temporarily modify $G_{A,O}$ to obtain $G'_{A,O}$, where the cost of each edge is its original cost in $G_{A,O}$ minus $c$. The cost of every cycle $C'$ in $G'_{A,O}$ is thus its cost in $G_{A,O}$ minus $c   |C'|$, which is greater than $\frac{cost_A(C')}{|C'|}   |C'| = cost_A(C')$, meaning there are no positive-cost cycles:
\[
cost_{A}(C') - c|C'| \leq cost_{A}(C') - \frac{cost_A(C')}{|C'|} |C'| = cost_A(C') - cost_A(C') = 0.
\]

By \Cref{thm:efable--iff-cost-cycles}, there exists a payment vector $\mathbf{p}$, that eliminates envy in $G'_{A,O}$. Using this payment vector, we have for each $i,j \in N$:
\begin{align*}
    & v_i(A_j) - v_i(o_j) + p_j \leq v_i(A_i) - v_i(o_i) + p_i + c \Longleftrightarrow \\ &v_i(A_j) - v_i(o_j) + p_j  - \left( v_i(A_i) - v_i(o_i) + p_i\right) \leq c
\end{align*}

