\begin{Lemma}
    There is no payment vector that achieves an envy bound lower than $MACC(G_{A,O})$.
\end{Lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $\mathbf{p}'$ be a payment vector, and let $C$ be the cycle with the maximum mean cost.

Assume by contradiction that $v_i(A_j) - v_i(o_j) + p_j' - \left( v_i(A_i) - v_i(o_i) + p_i'\right) < \frac{cost_A(C)}{|C|}$ for each $i, j \in N$. Then, 
\begin{align*} 
& cost_A(C) = \sum_{(i,j)\in C} \left( v_i(A_j)-v_i(o_j) - \left(v_i(A_i) - v_i(o_i)\right) \right) = \\& \sum_{(i,j)\in C} \left( v_i(A_j)-v_i(o_j) +p_j' - \left(v_i(A_i) - v_i(o_i) + p_i'\right) \right) < \\ & \sum_{(i,j)\in C} \frac{cost_A(C)}{|C|} = cost_A(C). 
\end{align*}
Thus, there is at least one agent with an envy of at least the maximum mean cost.
\end{proof}

\begin{Lemma}
The smallest maximum-envy attainable in a given allocation $A$ equals $MACC(G_{A,O})$.
\end{Lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $C$ be a cycle in $G_{A,O}$, and suppose its mean cost is $c$. 
By \Cref{lemm: cycle cost unchanged}, 
the cost of a cycle remains the same under any payment vector, and the same holds for the mean cost. 
By the pigeonhole principle, the cycle has at least one edge with cost at least $c$; this means that, for any payment vector, the maximum envy in $A$ is at least $c$.

By \Cref{lemm: envy bound mean cycle}, there exists a payment vector for which the envy of all agents is at most $c$.
