By \Cref{lemm: payment vector minimizes envy}, $\mathbf{p}(A)$ divides the attainable envy equally among all agents, meaning the maximum envy-sum for allocations $A$ and $B$ would be $\frac{ES(A)}{n}$ and $\frac{ES(B)}{n}$, respectively. Since $ES(A) \leq ES(B)$, this implies that the minimum maximum envy-sum is achieved by allocation $A$. It is important to note that for every allocation $A$, there exists at least one agent with envy-sum of at least $\frac{ES(A)}{n}$. Thus, by attaining a maximum envy-sum of $\frac{ES(A)}{n}$, we do not increase the maximum possible envy-sum.

We can find the allocation $A$ by solving the maximum-value bipartite matching problem between the agents and the items. This can be done in polynomial time in $n$. The computation of the payment vector $\mathbf{p}(A)$ is clearly polynomial in $n$.
