\begin{Theorem} \label{min ES mechanism}
For $O$, the original apartments, and $M$, the new apartments, an allocation $A$ and a balanced payment vector $\mathbf{p}(A)$ such that $(A,\mathbf{p}(A))$ is SEF can be found, or it can be determined that no such allocation and vector exist, all in polynomial time.
\end{Theorem}
\begin{proof}
    $(A,\mathbf{p}(A))$ is SEF if and only if $ES(A,\mathbf{p}(A))_i \leq 0$ for each agent $i\in N$. In other words, if $(A,\mathbf{p}(A))$ is not SEF, then for each agent $i\in N$, their envy sum bigger than 0: $$ES(A)_i = \frac{ES(A)}{n} > 0.$$ By \cref{lemm: payment vector minimizes envy}, $\mathbf{p}(A)$ is the optimal payment vector. Therefore, for any other payment vector $\mathbf{p}'(A)$, $(A,\mathbf{p}'(A))$ is not SEF, which means that $A$ is not SEF-able. 

    Based on \Cref{ES(A)}, for every other allocation $B$ that allocates exactly one item to each agent, its optimal payment vector is $\mathbf{p}(B)$, and for every agent $i\in N$, $$ES(B,\mathbf{p}(B)) = \frac{ES(B)}{n} \geq \frac{ES(A)}{n} > 0.$$
    This implies that $(B,\mathbf{p}(B))$ is not SEF, and since $\mathbf{p}(B)$ is the optimal payment vector, we can conclude that $B$ is not SEF-able too.
\end{proof}

As a result, we can define a new mechanism for finding an allocation and a payment vector that are SEF. We refer to this mechanism as the \emph{Minimum Envy Sum mechanism}:
%\erel{Maybe call it: Min-Max-Envy-Sum, as it minimizes the maximum envy-sum}.

\begin{Definition}
\label{def:min-envy-sum}
    The \emph{Minimum Envy Sum mechanism} consists of two parts:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item The mechanism finds the allocation $A$ that maximizes utilitarian welfare among all allocations in which each agent receives exactly one item.
        \item The mechanism calculates a payment vector $\mathbf{p}(A)$ by:
        \[
        p(A)_i = \frac{ES(A)_i}{n} - \frac{ES(A)}{n^2}.
        \]
    \end{enumerate}
\end{Definition}



