\section{Manipulations} \label{sec: manipulations}
In this section, we present an approach to gathering agents' valuations. In order to reduce the incentives of agents to manipulate their valuations for old and new apartments, 
rather than assessing the value of apartments based solely on unit values, we ask agents to assess the value of shared characteristics found in both old and new apartments — a common approach in real estate appraisal. Moreover, we analyze the conditions under which the Minimum Envy Sum mechanism (\Cref{def:min-envy-sum}) is free from 
%obvious or 
safe manipulation.

\paragraph{Truthfulness.}
The classical concept of truthfulness requires that no agent can benefit from misreporting their valuations—meaning there is no \emph{profitable manipulation}, an untruthful report that, under some circumstances, increases the agent’s utility. This definition assumes that a manipulating agent either knows the reports of all others or is willing to take the risk of acting as if they do.
To refine this notion, we use the definition of \emph{safe manipulation}, as an untruthful report that never reduces the agent’s utility. A mechanism is \emph{safely manipulable} if some agent has a manipulation that is both profitable and safe. If no such manipulation exists, the mechanism is said to be \emph{Risk-Avoiding Truthful (RAT)}.

A mechanism $\mathcal{M}$ takes reported valuations (which may differ from true valuations) as input and produces an outcome. Let $v_i(\mathcal{M}(v))$ denote the valuation of agent $i$ for their allocated outcome under $\mathcal{M}$.

\begin{Definition} 
A \emph{manipulation} for a mechanism $\mathcal{M}$ by an agent $i \in N$ is an untruthful report $v_i'$. It is \emph{profitable} if there exists a set of reports $v_{-i}$ from the other agents such that the agent gains a higher utility than by misreporting:
\[
\exists v_{-i} : v_i(\mathcal{M}(v_i', v_{-i})) > v_i(\mathcal{M}(v_i, v_{-i})) .
\]

A mechanism $\mathcal{M}$ is \emph{manipulable} if some agent has a profitable manipulation; otherwise, it is \emph{truthful}.
\end{Definition}

\begin{Definition}
    A manipulation is \emph{safe} if it never results in a worse outcome for the manipulator—i.e., the agent weakly prefers it over truthfulness for any possible reports of the other agents:
\[
\forall v_{-i} : v_i(\mathcal{M}(v_i', v_{-i})) \geq v_i(\mathcal{M}(v_i, v_{-i}))
\]
A mechanism $\mathcal{M}$ is \emph{safely manipulable} if some agent has a manipulation that is both profitable and safe. Otherwise, $\mathcal{M}$ is \emph{Risk-Avoiding Truthful (RAT)}.
\end{Definition}

\begin{Definition} A mechanism $\mathcal{M}$ is \emph{not obviously manipulable} (or simply \emph{NOM}) if for every agent $i \in N$ with valuation function $v_i$ and every possible report $v_i'$ of agent $i$, and reports of the other agents $v_{-i}$, the following two inequalities hold:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\displaystyle\min_{v_{-i}} v_i\left(\mathcal{M}_i\left(v_i,v_{-i}\right)\right) \geq \displaystyle\min_{v_{-i}}\left(\mathcal{M}_i\left(v_i', v_{-i}\right)\right)$.
        \item $\displaystyle\max_{v_{-i}} v_i\left(\mathcal{M}_i\left(v_i,v_{-i}\right)\right) \geq \displaystyle\max_{v_{-i}}\left(\mathcal{M}_i\left(v_i', v_{-i}\right)\right)$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{Definition}
Intuitively, a mechanism is considered \emph{NOM} if an agent cannot enhance her worst-case or best-case utility (as evaluated based on her true valuation) by misreporting her preferences. In other words, the best-case and worst-case utilities are maximized when the agent truthfully reports her valuation. If an agent can improve either her worst-case or best-case utility by misreporting, the mechanism is deemed \emph{obviously manipulable}.

\paragraph{Valuations as a Set of Characteristics.} Consider a collection of apartment characteristics, such as floor level, parking availability, airflow direction, and natural light. Each agent assigns a score to each characteristic. 

Let $K$ denote the set of characteristics.
For an apartment $a$ and a characteristic $c \in K$, define the indicator variable $\beta_{c,a}$, such that:%
\footnote{We use binary characteristics for simplicity. 
Also, in rebuild-and-divide projects, it is quite common to assess values of apartments coarsely using binary characteristics, such as "High floor", "renovated", etc.
}
\[
\beta_{c,a} =
\begin{cases}
     & 1, \text{if apartment } a \text{ possesses characteristic } c \\
    & 0, \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]

We consider two ways to combine the values of different characteristics.

\paragraph{(1) Additive Characteristics.}
Here we assume that the value of an apartment is the sum of the values of its characteristics. 
% Under this model, agents may have reduced incentives to manipulate their valuations, as truthful reporting aligns better with their preferences.

For each agent $i\in N$ and each characteristic $c\in K$, let $\alpha_{i,c}$ represent the value assigned by agent $i$ to characteristic $c$. 

The valuation of apartment $a$ from the respective of agent $i$ is then given by
\[
v_{i}(a) = \sum_{c\in K}\alpha_{i,c}\cdot \beta_{c,a}.
\]

