\subsection{Manipulations of the Minimum Envy Sum mechanism}
Recall that the Minimum Envy Sum mechanism (\Cref{def:min-envy-sum}) computes an allocation of the new apartments that maximizes the utilitarian welfare, and then computes a balanced payment vector $\mathbf{p}$ as follows:
\begin{align*}
    p_i &= \frac{ES(A)_i - \frac{ES(A)}{n}}{n} = \frac{n-1}{n^2} ES(A)_i - \sum_{j\neq i \in N}\frac{ 1}{n^2}ES(A)_j,
\end{align*}
where $ES(A)_j$ is, for each agent $j\in N$, the envy-sum felt by $j$ in allocation $A$.
Note that when the allocation is fixed, $ES(A)_j$ for each $j\neq i\in N$ does not depend on agent $i$'s valuation, whereas $ES(A)_i$ does.
Therefore, if a manipulation by $i$ does not change the allocation, but increases $ES(A)_i$, then it is profitable for $i$.

\begin{Observation}
    If, following a manipulation by agent $i$, the assignment does not change, then:

    (a) If the reported $v_i(o_i)$ increases, 
    then the utility of $i$ increases.

    (b) If the reported $v_i(o_j)$ decreases (for $j\neq i$),  then the utility of $i$ increases.
\end{Observation}


For brevity, throughout this section we denote the true valuation vector $(v_1,v_2,\ldots, v_n)$ as $v$, and the manipulated valuation vector, where only agent 1 misreports, as $v' = (v_1',v_2,\ldots, v_n)$.
We use $\mathbf{p}(v)$ to represent the payment vector under the valuation profile $v$, and let $ES(A,v)$ denote the envy-sum corresponding to allocation $A$ and valuation vector $v$.

Switching from evaluations of apartments to evaluations of characteristics does not necessarily make the mechanism RAT. For example, if the old apartment of agent $i$ has a characteristic $c$ that does not exist in any new apartment, then agent $i$ has a safe manipulation, namely increasing $v_i(c)$. This has no effect on the allocation (as the allocation only depends) on the new apartments' valuations), but it increases $v_i(o_i)$ and hence increases $ES_i(A)$ and increases $p_i$ (see \Cref{safely manipulable: old apartment unique characteristics} in the appendix).
There are other cases in which the mechanism is safely manipulable. For example, if a characteristic $c$ is found in $o_i$ and also in \emph{all} new apartments, then under the sum model, the allocation does not change whereas $p_i$ increases 
(see \Cref{safely manipulable: same characteristics} in the appendix).

\begin{toappendix}

\begin{Proposition}
\label{safely manipulable: old apartment unique characteristics}
When old apartments have unique characteristics that new apartments lack, the Minimum Envy Sum mechanism
% Give it a cool name
becomes safely manipulable. 

This is true both for additive and for multiplicative characteristics.
\end{Proposition}
\begin{proof}
 We consider $n$ agents with arbitrary valuation functions and $K$ characteristics, where $c_1\in K$ applies only to $o_1$. Without loss of generality, the allocation that maximizes social welfare is $A_i = \{a_i\}$ for each $i\in N$.

 As shown above, the payment for each agent $i$ is given by:
     $$p_i = \frac{n-1}{n^2} ES(A)_i - \sum_{j\neq i \in N}\frac{ 1}{n^2}ES(A)_j.$$
     Agent $1$ can benefit by misreporting their valuation, specifically by increasing the valuation of $c_1$. We denote the misreported valuation as $v'$. Since $c_1$ does not exist in the new apartments, this does not affect the allocation but does influence the payments in favor of Agent 1:

     \begin{align*}
         p(v')_1 = & \frac{n-1}{n^2}ES(A,v')_1 - \frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{j\neq 1\in N} ES(A,v')_j = 
         \\
         &
         \frac{n-1}{n^2}ES(A,v')_1 - \frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{j\neq 1\in N} ES(A,v)_j =
         \\
         &
         \frac{n-1}{n^2}\left(ES(A,v)_1 + n(v_1'(o_1) - v_1(o_1) ) \right) 
         - \frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{j\neq 1\in N} ES(A,v)_j.
         \end{align*}
    Because $n^2> n-1\geq 1$ and $v_1(o_1) < v_1'(o_1)$ we receive that
         \begin{align*}
         p(v')_1 >
         \frac{n-1}{n^2}ES(A,v)_1 - \frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{j\neq 1\in N} ES(A,v)_j = p(v)_1.
     \end{align*}
     Since the new allocation is not affected by the misreporting, no matter what the other agents report, agent 1 can benefit by misreporting their valuation, proving that the mechanism is safely manipulable.

     The proof for multiplicative characteristics is similar.
\end{proof}

\begin{Proposition}
\label{safely manipulable: same characteristics}
Under additive characteristics,
when old apartments have a characteristic that appears in all new apartments, the Minimum Envy Sum mechanism becomes safely manipulable.
\end{Proposition}

\begin{proof}
\underline{\bf Additive Characteristics:}
     We consider $n$ agents with arbitrary valuation functions and $K$ characteristics, where $c_1$ applies to $o_1, a_1, \ldots, a_n$ but not to $o_2,\ldots, o_n$. Without loss of generality, the allocation that maximizes social welfare is $A_i = \{a_i\}$ for each $i\in N$.

    As shown above, the payment for each agent $i$ is given by:
     $$p_i = \frac{n-1}{n^2}ES(A)_i - \frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{j\neq i\in N} ES(A)_j.$$
     Agent $1$ can benefit by misreporting their valuation, specifically by increasing the valuation of $c_1$ by $v_1'(c_1) > 0$. 
     Note that the envy of agent 1 towards agent $j\neq 1 \in N$ is 
     $$v_1'(A_j) - v_1'(o_j) - v_1'(A_1) + v_1'(o_1) = v_1(A_j) + v_1'(c_1) - v_1(o_j) - v_1(A_1) - v_1'(c_1) + v_1'(o_1) + v_1'(c_1).$$

     Since $c_1$ holds for every new apartment, increasing the value of $c_1$ results in increasing the values of all new apartments by the same amount, so the maximum-value allocation does not change. However,  the manipulation does affect the payments in favor of agent 1:
     \begin{align*}
         p(v')_1 = & \frac{n-1}{n^2}ES(A,v')_1 - \frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{j\neq 1\in N} ES(A,v')_j = \\
         & \frac{n-1}{n^2}ES(A,v')_1 - \frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{j\neq 1\in N} ES(A,v)_j = \\
         & \frac{n-1}{n^2}\left(ES(A,v)_1 + \sum_{j\neq 1\in N} \left( v_1'(c_1) - v_1'(c_1) + v_1'(c_1) \right) \right) - \frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{j\neq 1\in N} ES(A,v)_j > \\
         & \frac{n-1}{n^2}ES(A,v)_1 - \frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{j\neq 1\in N} ES(A,v)_j = p(v)_1.
     \end{align*}
     Since the misreporting does not change the allocation, regardless of what the other agents report, Agent 1 can still gain an advantage, demonstrating that the mechanism is safely manipulable.

\end{proof}




\underline{\bf Multiplicative Characteristics:}
We show a special case in which the above proof does not work.
Consider two agents, $ i_1 $ and $ i_2 $, and six characteristics, $ c_1, c_2, c_3, c_4, c_5, c_6 $. The values assigned by the agents to these characteristics are as follows:
\[
    \begin{bmatrix}
        & & c_1 & c_2 & c_3 & c_4 & c_5 & c_6\\
        &i_1 & 2 & 3 & 5 & 3 & 1 & 3 \\
        &i_2 & 1 & 2 & 2 & 2 & 4 & 8
    \end{bmatrix}
\]

The indicator variables for the apartments are defined as follows:
\[
    \begin{bmatrix}
        & & o_1 & o_2 & a_1 & a_2 \\
        &c_1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\
        &c_2 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\
        &c_3 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\
        &c_4 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\
        &c_5 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\
        &c_6 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 
    \end{bmatrix}
\]
The base prices of the apartments are as follows:
\[
    \begin{bmatrix}
        & & \makecell{\text{Base Price}} \\
        & o_1 &  1 \\
        & o_2 &  1 \\
        & a_1 &  1 \\
        & a_2 &  1
    \end{bmatrix}
\]
That is, the total values for each apartment are as follows:
\[
    \begin{bmatrix}
        & & o_1 & o_2 & a_1 & a_2 \\
        &v_1 & 2 & 3 & 10 & 18 \\
        &v_2 & 1 & 2 & 8 & 16
    \end{bmatrix}
\]

The allocation where agent $i_1$ receives apartment $a_2$ and agent $i_2$ receives apartment $a_1$ yields the same total utility as the allocation where agent $i_1$ receives apartment $a_2$ and agent $i_2$ receives apartment $a_1$:
\[
v_1(a_1) + v_2(a_2) = 10 + 16 = 26 = 18 + 8 = v_1(a_2) + v_2(a_1).
\]

Without loss of generality, we can assume that agent $i_1$ receives apartment $a_2$ and agent $i_2$ receives apartment $a_1$ in this optimal allocation.

The payment agent $i_1$ pays under this allocation is:
\[
p(v)_1 = \frac{10 - 3 - 18 + 2}{4} - \frac{16 - 1 - 8 + 2}{4} = \frac{-9}{4} - \frac{9}{4} = -\frac{9}{2},
\]
and the utility of agent 1 is:
\[
u(v)_1 = v_1(a_2) + p(v)_1 = 18 - \frac{9}{2} = 13 + \frac{1}{2}.
\]

Assume agent 1 reports $v'(c_1) = x$ as the valuation of characteristic $ c_1 $. Then the new values become:
\[
    \begin{bmatrix}
        & & o_1 & o_2 & a_1 & a_2 \\
        &v_1' &x & 3 & 5x & 9x \\
        &v_2 & 1 & 2 & 8 & 16
    \end{bmatrix}
\]
There are two cases to consider:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{The new allocation remains the same.}
    In this case,
    \begin{align*}
        & v_1'(a_1) + v_2(a_2) \leq v_1'(a_2) + v_2(a_1) \Leftrightarrow
        5x + 16 \leq 9x + 8 \Leftrightarrow 8 \leq 4x \Leftrightarrow 2 \leq x.
    \end{align*}
    We say that, in order to keep the allocation unchanged, we need $2 < x$; otherwise agent $i_1$ does not change the valuation at all.

The payment agent 1 receives is:
\[
\begin{aligned}
    p(v')_1 &= \frac{v_1'(a_1) - v_1'(o_2) - v_1'(a_2) + v_1'(o_1)}{4} - \frac{v_2(a_2) - v_2(o_1) - v_2(a_1) + v_2(o_1)}{4} \\
    &= \frac{5x - 3 - 9x + x}{4} - \frac{9}{4} = \frac{-3x - 12}{4} < \frac{-6 -12 }{4} = -\frac{9}{2}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Thus, the utility of agent 1 is:
\[
u(v')_1 = v_1(a_2) + p(v')_1 < 18 - \frac{9}{2} = u(v)_1.
\]

\item \textbf{The new allocation changes.} In this case, $x < 2$.

The payment agent 1 receives is:
\[
\begin{aligned}
    p(v')_1 &= \frac{v_1'(a_2) - v_1'(o_2) - v_1'(a_1) + v_1'(o_1)}{4} - \frac{v_2(a_1) - v_2(o_1) - v_2(a_2) + v_2(o_1)}{4} \\
    &= \frac{9x - 3 - 5x + x}{4} - \frac{8 - 1 - 16 + 2}{4} = \frac{5x - 3}{4} - \frac{-7}{4} = \frac{5x + 4}{4} \\
    &< \frac{10 + 4}{4} = \frac{7}{2} .
\end{aligned}
\]
Thus, the utility of agent 1 is:
\[
u(v')_1 = v_1(a_1) + p(v')_1 < 10 + \frac{7}{2} < 13 + \frac{1}{2} = u(v)_1.
\]
\end{enumerate}

In both cases, agent 1 risks decreasing his utility. Thus, the mechanism is risk-avoiding truthful.


