\\
& = \frac{2v_1(a_1)  - 2v_1(a_2) + 2v_2(a_2) - 2v_2(a_2) + z}{4} \\
& = \frac{(v_1(a_1) + v_2(a_2)) - (v_1(a_2) + v_2(a_1))}{2}
\\
& =  \alpha > 0.           
\end{align*}
That is, the utility of agent 1 decreases due to the manipulation.

\textbf{Note:} This proposition does not extend to the case in which there are unknown characteristics in only one apartment. To understand why, consider the case where $ c \in K(o_1) $ and $ c \notin K(o_2) $. If agent~1 knows that $ c \notin K(a_2) $ but does not know whether $c\in K(a_1)$ or not, 
