If the matching changes from (1-a2,2-a1) to (1-a1,2-a2), and 
    v2(c1) + v2(c4) > v2(c5) + v2(c2),
    [that is: agent 2 prefers the old matching (1-a2,2-a1)]
then agent 1 loses from the manipulation.

Consider a potential manipulation for agent 1, from v1(c1) to v1'(c1) > v1(c1).

Suppose the other reports satisfy:

1. v2(c1) + v2(c4) > v2(c5) + v2(c2),
2. [v1(c2)+v1(c5)+v2(c1)+v2(c4) - v1(c4)-v2(c2)-v2(c5)] = [v1(c1) + v1'(c1)]/2

v1(c2)+v1(c5)-v1(c4)
+[v2(c1)+v2(c4)-v2(c2)-v2(c5)]

Then, by condition 1:

            v1(c1) 
            < [v1(c2)+v1(c5)+v2(c1)+v2(c4) - v1(c4)-v2(c2)-v2(c5)]
            < v'1(c1).

So:
              v1(c1)+v1(c4)+v2(c2)+v2(c5) 
            < v1(c2)+v1(c5)+v2(c1)+v2(c4) 
            < v1'(c1)+v1(c4)+v2(c2)+v2(c5)

So without the manipulation, the matching is (1-a2,2-a1);
with the manipulation, the matching is (1-a1,2-a2);
and the utility of agent 1 is lower.

PROBLEM: this assumes that agent 1 manipulates only the value of c1.
But, agent 1 can also manipulate the other properties...

The matching changes if and only if

v1(c1)+v1(c4)-v1(c2)-v1(c5) 
< v2(c1)+v2(c4)-v2(c2)-v2(c5)
< v1'(c1)+v'1(c4)-v'1(c2)-v'1(c5) 




\fi
