\section{Conclusions and Future Work}
In this paper, we studied the practical problem of achieving envy-freeness in Rebuild-and-Redivide settings. We introduced three models for capturing envy—(1) \textbf{the difference model}, (2) the \textbf{envy-sum model}, and (3) the \textbf{ratio model}—and characterized when envy-free allocations exist in each. Since such allocations are not always guaranteed, we focused on minimizing envy through allocation and payment vectors.

In the \textbf{difference model}, we showed that the maximum attainable envy equals the maximum average mean cycle cost in the difference envy-graph. However, with $n!$ possible allocations, no known polynomial-time algorithm can guarantee an optimal solution. Whether such an algorithm exists remains an open question.

For the \textbf{envy-sum model}, we proposed a mechanism that minimizes the maximum envy sum across agents. To reduce strategic manipulation in reporting, we introduced a novel elicitation method based on shared apartment characteristics and explored two aggregation methods for combining characteristic values. We also identified conditions under which the mechanism is resistant to obvious or safe manipulations. Extending these results to broader settings and identifying additional manipulation-resistant conditions remains an open direction.

Lastly, for the \textbf{ratio model}, while it is conceptually appealing, our current results are limited. Developing deeper theoretical insights in this model is a key area for future research.


