\section{dKS as an Integral Probability Measure}
\label{sec:IPM+RS}

In this section we make an argument that the proposed dKS formulation using only one-quadrant ranges ($\RR_d$, not all $2^d$ options) is perfectly reasonable.  As an implication, due to its efficiency and invariance to units along its axes, should be a preferred choice in many context.   
To make this ``reasonableness'' claim we show that $\dKS$ is from a well-studied distance family (where as other variants are not).  

