\section{Empirical Performance for d = 2}
\label{sec:epirical}

To empirically show the improved runtime of the convergence of $\dKS(\mu,\nu)$ we run a simple demonstration in the $d=2$ setting.  Since the results should not depend on the distributions, we keep things simple and set $\mu = \nu = \mathsf{Unif}([0,1]^2)$; the uniform distribution over the unit square.  Thus we know $\dKS(\mu,\nu) =0$, and so the observed error is just the value $\hat v$ estimated by the algorithm.  We increase the size of the samples drawn from $\mu, \nu$ (using $n$ to denote the size of each).  In Figure \ref{fig:plots} we measure and plot the runtime and the error as a function of $n$.   All run times and error measurements are shown as the average over 20 samples.  


\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{plots.pdf}
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{plots-big.pdf}
    \caption{Plots showing runtime and observed error as a function of the sample size $n$ for the {\sffamily Baseline} and {\sffamily Our Algo} on a simple $d=2$ example. The top plots show up to $n=16{,}384$ and the bottom ones show up to $n = 131{,}072$.}
    \label{fig:plots}
\end{figure}


We compare two algorithms, our proposed algorithm {\sffamily Our Algo} from Section \ref{ssec:algo} which runs in $O(n \log n)$ time, as well as the {\sffamily Baseline} from the start of Section \ref{sec:runtime} which runs in $O(n^2)$ time.  We see ours has runtime increase near-linearly in $n$, while the baseline increases quadratically.  With $n=16,384$, ours takes under 0.2 seconds, and the baseline already takes about 3.5 seconds.  Going up to $n=131{,}072$ (so observed error is less than $0.005$), then ours takes only about 1 second, while the baseline takes more than $5$ minutes.  
Moreover, ours does not exhibit much difference in error as a function of $n$, compared to the baseline; they appear to converge at the same rate.  



\subparagraph{Discussion on Future Directions.}
Our implementation is simple python code, and has not yet been fully optimized, or abstracted so we can more generally use its full metric properties (e.g., for clustering distributions).  While we believe our simple experiment is representative of the runtime effects, the optimization, abstraction, and use of dKS in broader applications could result in a more thorough evaluation.    Continuing this, we hope to promote this method, and algorithm, so this distance measure between distributions is more widely used -- especially in applications where there are not consistent units among coordinates.  

Moreover, the described algorithms for $d=3,4$ which are near-linear in $n$ may not be simple to implement since they rely on a reduction to a recursive procedure for Klee's measure problem~\cite{chan2013klee} -- despite this approach having been ``made easy'' in comparison to prior solutions.  Yet, we are hopeful that there are even simpler and more practical solutions for $d > 2$ that also achieve near-linear runtime in $n$.  In particular, for $d=3$, the Klee's measure part only takes $\tilde O(1/\eps^{3/2})$ time, so there should be room to trade-off in theoretical runtime for practical improvements.  



