Narrowband speech signals suffer from high-frequency loss due to transmission limitations, leading to reduced clarity and naturalness. Speech super-reolution (SR) aims to reconstruct these missing frequencies, extending narrowband speech to full-band and enhancing speech quality for applications such as hearing aids, voice communication, and automatic speech recognition systems. While most existing SBE solutions focus primarily on recovering the magnitude response, recent studies emphasize the critical role of phase reconstruction in improving perceptual speech quality. Motivated by multi-task learning, this study proposes a Complex Time-Frequency Transformation network (CTFT-Net) for complex spectral mapping, enabling simultaneous reconstruction of both magnitude and phase responses. The proposed network integrates a Complex Global Attention Block (CGAB) in the encoder to model inter-phoneme and inter-frequency dependencies, enhancing feature extraction. Additionally, a Conformer block in the bottleneck layer captures long-range dependencies and local features, improving frequency reconstruction and noise robustness. The model further employs time-domain and multi-resolution frequency-domain loss functions, increasing its generalization capacity across both domains. Experimental results demonstrate that CTFT-Net outperforms existing models such as Super Denoise Net (SDNet) and AERO in terms of objective speech intelligibility and quality. Notably, the model achieves significant performance improvements for extremely band-limited signals with upsampling ratios of 2, 4, and 8, effectively reconstructing high-frequency components without introducing musical artifacts commonly observed in conventional methods.
