Previously, researchers trained deep neural networks (DNNs) to model the nonlinear relationship between low- and high-frequency components to perform the SSR tasks. With the increase of computational hardware and the wide adaptation of generative neural networks, the performance of SSR models has increased significantly. Auto Encoders (AE)[cite some papers here], Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs)[ ], Diffussion Models (DMs)[ ], and Neural Vocoders [ ] have achieved some greatest feats in SSR which was previously unimaginable. These generative models deploy multi stage or end-to-end training which improves the audio quality in magnitude and phase domain by processing audio waveforms or spectrograms. 

However, there still some notable challenges yet to be addressed. One of the most challenging tasks is to faithfully reconstruct audios from very low frequency (<1KHz) in noisy environments. Although traditional SSR models predict the low frequency frequencies pretty accurately, they struggle to predict the high frequencies [cite]. Due to the delicate timbres, without the accurate prediction of the higher order harmonics, the generated audio quality will be very bad and sometimes will not be intelligible at all.

There are three main approaches in which audio data is processed: waveforms, magnitude spectrograms, and complex numbers. Although waveform representation offers end-to-end training, it is very difficult to model longer sequence lengths. Magnitude spectrograms offer a more compact representation, however, due to the requirement of multistage processing and loss of phase information which is very crucial in the reconstruction [cite], the performance and capability of the spectrogram-based models are quite limited. The complex representation solves the limitations of previous two approaches by leveraging the inherent capabilities of complex numbers to potentially represent both the magnitude and phase information simultaneously and using complex Neural Network to improve the performance in both domain [cite].

There exists strong correlation between the low frequency components and high frequency components [cite]. Sometimes, the high frequency components are just a higher order harmonics of the fundamental low frequencies. To capture this relationship, authors [ ] devised Frequency Transformation Block (FTB) and showed the increased performance of the models by effectively modeling this relationship. We have modified the real FTB to complex FTB to capture the intricate frequency relationship present in both frequency and time domain.  Simultaneously inspiring by this simple and ingenious idea we were curious about the inter phoneme dependencies present across the time dimension. We developed a novel complex Time Transformation Block (TTB) to mimic the functionalities of FTB across time axis for both magnitude and phase information. We have also combined FTB and TTB blocks in various combinations (in series or in parallel) and find out the best set of combinations by extensive ablation studies. 

Previous research has showed that widely used activation functions like ReLU, Sigmoid, etc failed to capture the periodic induction bias [ ]. To mitigate this issue, authors [ ] developed a trigonometric sinusoidal operation based snake activation function. Inspired by this, we have developed complex snake function, the first ever implementation of snake activation function in the complex domain. 

Loss functions plays a very important role in the performance of the neural networks. Most of the previous works [ , , , ] only uses loss function in one specific domain either time domain or in frequency domain. Our intuition was that if we could minimize the losses both in frequency domain and time domain then we will get a better reconstructed audio. For this reason we have choosen time domain SI-SDR loss and frequency domain single resolution and multi resolution STFT loss. Multi resolution STFT loss ensures to capture both fine-grained and coarsed grained information across multiple resolutions by trading off between better time resolution and better frequency resolution. As expected, the first ever implemented complex multi resolution STFT loss outperforms the single resolution loss. 

Bottleneck layer is perhaps one of the most important part of the UNet based architecture as the most prominent salient extracted features are passed to decoder from the encoder by this layer. To effectively capture this, we tried and used various combinations of GRU, Transformer, Conformer, and Lattice Block. To best of our knowledge this work is the first implementation of these architectures in complex domain.

By integrating these innovations, our framework not only overcomes the challenges associated with very low frequency BWE tasks but also outperforms current state-of-the-art architectures across a variety of experimental conditions. One of our primary aims was to enhance perceptual quality by expanding the bandwidth of the filtered audio while still preserving its intelligibility. Typically, the perceptual quality of band-limited audio is diminished because high formants are absent in low frequencies due to nasal obstruction. Moreover, extensive ablation studies on the VCTK-smaller dataset have confirmed the effectiveness of each innovative component, as measured by LSD, STOI, PESQ, and SI-SDR metrics.

