\subsection{Feature Extraction}
The Short-Time Fourier Transform (STFT) is computed by first constructing a convolutional kernel that represents a scaled Fourier basis modulated by a square-root Hann window. This kernel is obtained by taking the real and imaginary parts of an identity matrix and scaling it with the square-root Hann window to ensure perfect reconstruction.

Let \( x[n] \) be the input time-domain signal, and let \( w[n] \) denote the window function defined as:
\begin{align}
w[n] = \sqrt{\text{hann}(n)}, \quad n = 0, 1, \dots, N-1,
\end{align}

where \( N \) is the frame length, \( H \) is the frame hop size, and \( F \) is the FFT size. The STFT is then given by:
\begin{align}
X[k, m] = \sum_{n=0}^{N-1} x[n + mH]\, w[n]\, e^{-j \frac{2\pi k n}{F}},
\end{align}
where \( m \) indexes the time frames and \( k \) indexes the frequency bins.

The magnitude and phase of \( X[k, m] \) are computed as:
\begin{align}
|X[k, m]| = \sqrt{(\Re\{X[k, m]\})^2 + (\Im\{X[k, m]\})^2},
\end{align}
\begin{align}
\angle X[k, m] = \arctan\left(\frac{\Im\{X[k, m]\}}{\Re\{X[k, m]\}}\right).
\end{align}

For the inverse STFT (iSTFT), the process is reversed. Given the magnitude \( M[k, m] \) and phase \( P[k, m] \), the real and imaginary parts of the complex coefficients are reconstructed by:
\begin{align}
\Re\{X[k, m]\} = M[k, m] \cos(P[k, m]),
\end{align}
\begin{align}
\Im\{X[k, m]\} = M[k, m] \sin(P[k, m]).
\end{align}
These reconstructed coefficients are then mapped back to the time domain via a transposed convolution, which essentially performs an overlap-add synthesis. The reconstructed time-domain signal \( \tilde{x}[n] \) can be interpreted as:
\begin{align}
\tilde{x}[n] = \sum_{m} w[n - mH] \left( \sum_{k} X[k, m]\, e^{j \frac{2\pi k (n-mH)}{F}} \right).
\end{align}

A normalization factor \( S \) is also applied during the kernel initialization (with \( S = 0.5\sqrt{\frac{F^2}{H}} \)) to ensure that the windowing and the overlap-add synthesis lead to perfect reconstruction. This careful design using a square-root Hann window guarantees that the STFT followed by the iSTFT accurately reconstructs the original signal.

\subsection{Deepfiltering }

Deepfiltering is a key post processing component of the proposed framework. It is used to refine the complex generated spectrogram obtained via the inverse Short-Time Fourier Transform (iSTFT) and to suppress noise while preserving the speech content. In our model, deepfiltering applies a learned complex-valued filter over local neighborhoods in the time-frequency domain. This operation enhances the generated complex spectrogram by adaptively combining information from shifted copies of the input, thereby exploiting local contextual correlations in both time and frequency domain.
\begin{align}
X \in \mathbb{C}^{B \times F \times T}
\end{align}
denote the complex spectrogram of the input signal (with batch size \(B\), \(F\) frequency bins, and \(T\) time frames). The network learns a deep filter 
\begin{align}
W \in \mathbb{C}^{B \times F \times T \times D},
\end{align}
where \(D\) corresponds to the number of local shifts defined by predetermined frequency taps \(\mathcal{F}\) and time taps \(\mathcal{T}\).

The deepfiltering process involves the following steps:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Permuting the Filter:} The filter tensor is reshaped so that the shift-dimension is last,
    \begin{align}
    W' = \mathrm{permute}(W, [0,2,3,1]),
    \end{align}
    ensuring that for each time-frequency bin \((f,t)\) a set of \(D\) filter coefficients is available.

    \item \textbf{Generating Shifted Inputs:} For each shift \(d\) corresponding to frequency and time offsets \((\Delta f_d, \Delta t_d)\) (with \(\Delta f_d \in \mathcal{F}\) and \(\Delta t_d \in \mathcal{T}\)), a shifted version of the input is generated. Using the roll operator, we obtain
    \begin{align}
    X_{f,t,d} = \mathrm{roll}(X, (\Delta f_d, \Delta t_d)),
    \end{align}
    and collect all these shifted copies into a tensor
    \begin{align}
    X_{\text{shift}} \in \mathbb{C}^{B \times F \times T \times D}.
    \end{align}

    \item \textbf{Weighted Aggregation:} The enhanced spectrogram \(\hat{X}\) is computed as a weighted sum over the local neighborhood:
    \begin{align}
    \hat{X}(f,t) = \sum_{d=1}^{D} \overline{W'}(f,t,d) \, X_{\text{shift}}(f,t,d),
    \end{align}
    where \(\overline{W'}(f,t,d)\) denotes the complex conjugate of the filter coefficient. In our implementation, this summation is efficiently carried out using Einstein summation notation:
    \begin{align}
    \hat{X} = \text{einsum}\bigl('bftd,bfdt \to bft',\, \overline{W'},\, X_{\text{shift}}\bigr).
    \end{align}
\end{enumerate}


Deepfiltering is used for several important reasons:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Localized Enhancement:} By aggregating information from a local neighborhood (via shifts in frequency and time), the network can better distinguish between speech components and noise.
    \item \textbf{Complex-Domain Processing:} Operating directly on complex spectrograms allows simultaneous enhancement of magnitude and phase, which is essential for high-fidelity signal reconstruction.
    \item \textbf{Adaptive Filtering:} The filter coefficients \(W\) are learned in a data-driven manner. This adaptation enables the model to handle a variety of frequency components present in the enhanced generated audio.
    \item \textbf{Computational Efficiency:} Utilizing tensor operations (such as \texttt{torch.roll} and \texttt{einsum}) enables an efficient implementation that is suitable for faster computation.
\end{itemize}

Overall, deepfiltering leverages a learned complex filter to perform an adaptive convolution-like operation in the time-frequency domain. This approach is critical to our method’s ability to enhance speech signals while effectively suppressing unwanted noise.

\subsection{Complex Multi-Resolution STFT Loss}
In this work, we propose a novel \emph{Complex Multi-Resolution STFT Loss} that measures the differences between clean and enhanced signals in the complex time–frequency domain. Unlike standard magnitude-only losses, our loss function separately evaluates errors on the real and imaginary components of the short-time Fourier transform (STFT) across multiple resolutions, thereby capturing both fine and coarse spectral details in complex domain.

Let \( x(t) \) and \(\hat{x}(t)\) represent the target and generated time-domain signals, respectively. For a given STFT configuration indexed by \( s \) with parameters \(\{n_{\mathrm{fft}}^s, h^s, w^s\}\) (FFT size, hop size, and window length), we compute
\begin{align}
X^s &= \operatorname{STFT}(x(t); n_{\mathrm{fft}}^s, h^s, w^s), \\
\hat{X}^s &= \operatorname{STFT}(\hat{x}(t); n_{\mathrm{fft}}^s, h^s, w^s).
\end{align}


\subsubsection{Spectral Convergence Loss}
The spectral convergence loss quantifies the relative error between the target and generated STFT components. For the real and imaginary parts, it is defined as
\begin{align}
  L_{\mathrm{SC}}^{s, r} = \frac{\lVert X^s_{r} - \hat{X}^s_{r} \rVert_F}{\lVert X^s_{r} \rVert_F + \epsilon}, \quad
  L_{\mathrm{SC}}^{s, i} = \frac{\lVert X^s_{i} - \hat{X}^s_{i} \rVert_F}{\lVert X^s_{i} \rVert_F + \epsilon},
\end{align}
where \(\lVert \cdot \rVert_F\) denotes the Frobenius norm and \(\epsilon\) is a small constant to ensure numerical stability.

\subsubsection{Log-Magnitude Loss}
To account for perceptual differences in spectral content, log-magnitude loss, defined separately for the real and imaginary components:
\begin{align}
  L_{\mathrm{mag}}^{s, r} = \Big\lVert \log\big(|X^s_{r}| + \epsilon\big) - \log\big(|\hat{X}^s_{r}| + \epsilon\big) \Big\rVert_1,
\end{align}
\begin{align}
  L_{\mathrm{mag}}^{s, i} = \Big\lVert \log\big(|X^s_{i}| + \epsilon\big) - \log\big(|\hat{X}^s_{i}| + \epsilon\big) \Big\rVert_1,
\end{align}
where \(\lVert \cdot \rVert_1\) is the \(L_1\)-norm. This term penalizes differences in the logarithmic magnitude spectrum, which is known to be perceptually relevant for audio quality.

\subsubsection{Multi-Resolution Aggregation}
Assuming we have \( S \) different STFT resolutions (i.e., different sets of \(\{n_{\mathrm{fft}}, h, w\}\)), we aggregate the losses by averaging over the resolutions for both real and imaginary parts:
\begin{align}
  L_{\mathrm{real}} = \frac{1}{S} \sum_{s=1}^{S} \Big( L_{\mathrm{SC}}^{s, r} + L_{\mathrm{mag}}^{s, r} \Big),
\end{align}
\begin{align}
  L_{\mathrm{imag}} = \frac{1}{S} \sum_{s=1}^{S} \Big( L_{\mathrm{SC}}^{s, i} + L_{\mathrm{mag}}^{s, i} \Big).
\end{align}
The overall complex STFT loss is then given by the sum of the real and imaginary losses:
\begin{align}
  L_{\mathrm{c}} = L_{\mathrm{real}} + L_{\mathrm{imag}}.
\end{align}

This loss formulation simultaneously enforces spectral fidelity in both magnitude and phase components across multiple time–frequency scales, which has been shown to be beneficial in SSR tasks. The multi-resolution aspect allows the model to capture fine-grained details as well as broader spectral structures, leading to improved reconstruction quality.

