\textbf{Post processing and snake activation:} We experiment with the NVSR-style post-processing technique (line $P_3$) discussed in \cite{liu2022neural}. Line $P_{3}$ indicates that CTFT-Net gives better results with the NVSR-style post-processing. However, we don't use any post-processing in our current design to prove that CTFT-Net works much better even without any post-processing. Moreover, our model gives better results with simpler ReLU activation compared to snake activation used in \cite{mandel2023aero} (see line $P_4$).




%\textbf{Complex and real MR-STFT loss:} The MR-STFT loss is better compared to single-resolution STFT loss for CTFT-Net (see lines $P_6$ \& $P_{10}$) because it can capture fine and coarse-grained details from different resolutions. Moreover, complex MR-STFT, $L^{cmplx}_{MR-STFT}$, can be calculated by jointly optimizing real and imaginary parts of STFT. Formally, $L^{cmplx}_{MR-STFT}$ = $L_r$ + $L_i$, where $L_r$  and $L_i$ are defined as (see Section \ref{subsec:Complex Multi-Resolution STFT Loss}):

\begin{comment}
\vspace{-0.75em}
{\footnotesize
\begin{align}
  L_{\mathrm{r}} = \frac{1}{S} \sum_{s=1}^{S} \Big( L_{\mathrm{SC}}^{r} + L_{\mathrm{mag}}^{r} \Big);
  L_{\mathrm{i}} = \frac{1}{S} \sum_{s=1}^{S} \Big( L_{\mathrm{SC}}^{i} + L_{\mathrm{mag}}^{i} \Big)
\end{align}
}
\vspace{-0.5em}
\end{comment}

%The complex MR-STFT loss typically gives worse results (see line $P_7$) compared to real MR-STFT loss as the SI-SDR loss makes $L_{i}$ redundant.

\textbf{Real single-resolution STFT (SR-STFT) loss:} We experiment with real-valued SR-STFT loss for different values of \{frequency bins, hop sizes, window lengths\}. Experiments find that the real-valued MR-STFT loss is always better compared to real-valued SR-STFT loss for CTFT-Net because MR-STFT loss can capture fine and coarse-grained details from different resolutions. $P_{5}$ shows the real-valued SR-STFT loss for \{frequency bins, hop sizes, window lengths\} = \{320, 80, 320\}. We define real-valued SR-STFT loss, $L^r_{\mathrm{SR-STFT}}$,  as:

\vspace{-01.0em}
{
\begin{align}
\vspace{-0.5em}
  L^r_{\mathrm{SR-STFT}} =  L_{\mathrm{SC}}^{r} + L_{\mathrm{mag}}^{r}
\end{align}
}
\vspace{-0.85em}

where $L_{\mathrm{SC}}^{r}$ and  $L_{\mathrm{mag}}^{r}$ are real-parts of the spectral convergence loss \cite{tian2020tfgan} and log magnitude loss \cite{tian2020tfgan}, respectively.



%\textbf{Transformer and lattice blocks:} We study the inclusion of transformers \cite{ashish2017attention} and lattice blocks \cite{luo2020latticenet} by replacing conformers in bottleneck layers of our CTFT-Net. Lines $P_8$ \& $P_9$ show that a conformer is superior than transformers and lattice blocks in speech tasks because of its joint local and global attention.

%\textbf{Transformer blocks:} We study the inclusion of transformers \cite{ashish2017attention} by replacing conformers in bottleneck layers of our CTFT-Net. Lines $P_8$ \& $P_{10}$ show that a conformer gives better results compared to transformers because of its joint local and global attention in speech tasks.


\textbf{Remarks:} As CTFT-Net is trained with fixed input resolutions, it is not tested other than the same input audio resolution. Moreover, CTFT-Net is not evaluated on other than speech datasets (i.e., music, etc.) as our goal is SSR.


\vspace{-0.5em}
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
\vspace{-0.3em}

This paper presents a novel SSR framework that operates entirely in complex domains, jointly reconstructing both magnitude and phase from the LR signal using global attention on T-F axes.  It shows strong performance across a wide range of input sampling rates ranging from 2 kHz to 48 kHz. For the VCTK multi-speaker benchmark, results show that CTFT-Net outperforms the SOTA SSR models.


\begin{comment}
The ablation study detailed in Table 3 offers a refined insight into how each component individually contributes to the overall performance. It systematically demonstrates the impact of integrating complex modules such as FTB, TTB, Transformer, Conformer, Snake, and the Multi Resolution STFT loss (MRFL), along with their respective placements within the network. Notably, the architecture variant “C-UNet + FTB + Transformer + MRFL + Snake” delivers the highest PESQ (1.7337), while “C-UNet + TTB + Transformer + MRFL” achieves the top SI-SDR (14.7603). Among the various configurations, the “C-UNet + Parallel FTTB + Conformer + MRFL” model, which has 15.6 MB of parameters, finds an optimal balance by attaining the lowest LSD (2.1594) along with competitive STOI (0.8300) and PESQ (1.5763) scores, all while keeping the model size moderate. In contrast, the more parameter-heavy variant “C-UNet + Parallel FTTB in each encoder decoder + Conformer + MRFL” (52.9 MB) only provides slight STOI improvements and does not surpass the optimal configuration in LSD or PESQ, indicating that increased complexity yields diminishing returns. The findings further indicate that incorporating a Multi Resolution loss outperforms single resolution STFT losses, whereas substituting the Complex ReLU with the Complex Snake activation function results in varied metric effects. Overall, the “C-UNet + Parallel FTTB + Conformer + MRFL” variant demonstrates superior performance across evaluation metrics, underscoring the importance of balanced design choices within a sophisticated combination of components to achieve high-quality audio reconstruction.



